"use strict";(self.webpackChunkglobal_blog=self.webpackChunkglobal_blog||[]).push([["9867"],{46642(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"2026-06-18-maeumjigi-23","metadata":{"permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-06-18-maeumjigi-23","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2026-06-18-maeumjigi-23/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-06-18-maeumjigi-23/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #23] Safety Wrapper & 1st Semester Final Presentation","description":"Cover Image","date":"2026-06-18T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"fine-tuning","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/fine-tuning"},{"inline":true,"label":"safety wrapper","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/safety-wrapper"},{"inline":true,"label":"cloud","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/cloud"},{"inline":true,"label":"capstone","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/capstone"}],"readingTime":1.38,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"jaybee","title":"Maeumjigi Developer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-06-18-maeumjigi-23","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #23] Safety Wrapper & 1st Semester Final Presentation","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","fine-tuning","safety wrapper","cloud","capstone"],"date":"2026-06-18T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"]},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #22] 4th Fine-Tuning Complete! Architecture Design & Final Report","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-30-maeumjigi-22"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nAs we entered June, the Maeumjigi team completed the major developments of the 1st semester Capstone project and focused entirely on building **safety nets** and preparing for the **final presentation**.\\r\\n\\r\\n### The Victory of the 2:1 Ratio and the Safety Wrapper\\r\\n\\r\\n> \\"What was our chosen model?\\"\\r\\n> \\"We decided on 2:1. Now design a method to use a safety_wrapper as a safety lock for our Main Large Language Model (LLM) (2:1).\\"\\r\\n\\r\\nAs a result of the 4th fine-tuning, we selected the model mixed with a 2:1 ratio of \'emotional\' to \'daily\' chat as our final LLM. However, given the nature of a suicide prevention chatbot, we could not allow even a single hallucination or fatal wrong answer.\\r\\n\\r\\nTo prevent this, we introduced a **Safety Wrapper**. It verifies the input and output of the LLM, and if a high-risk situation (Active Suicidality) is detected, the architecture instantly switches to a pre-defined crisis response protocol.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Cloud Server (EC2) Budget and Final Presentation\\r\\n\\r\\n> \\"We need an AWS EC2 quote. What\'s the budget for 5 months of g5.2xlarge?\\"\\r\\n> \\"Draft the 1st_semester_final_presentation.pptx based on our progress!\\"\\r\\n\\r\\n![Final Presentation](./images/02_presentation.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nWe calculated the Amazon EC2 GPU server budget to run the verified chatbot for 5 months during the summer vacation.\\r\\n\\r\\nAnd on June 18th, we gathered all the achievements of our backend, frontend, and Artificial Intelligence (AI) to complete the **1st Semester Final Presentation (PPTX)**!\\r\\n\\r\\nThe long and arduous 1st semester of the Maeumjigi Capstone development has successfully concluded. We will deploy the system to the server over the summer and return with a more advanced service in the 2nd semester!"},{"id":"2026-05-30-maeumjigi-22","metadata":{"permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-30-maeumjigi-22","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2026-05-30-maeumjigi-22/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-30-maeumjigi-22/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #22] 4th Fine-Tuning Complete! Architecture Design & Final Report","description":"Cover Image","date":"2026-05-30T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"fine-tuning","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/fine-tuning"},{"inline":true,"label":"architecture","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/architecture"},{"inline":true,"label":"capstone","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/capstone"},{"inline":true,"label":"final report","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/final-report"}],"readingTime":1.35,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"jaybee","title":"Maeumjigi Developer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-05-30-maeumjigi-22","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #22] 4th Fine-Tuning Complete! Architecture Design & Final Report","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","fine-tuning","architecture","capstone","final report"],"date":"2026-05-30T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #23] Safety Wrapper & 1st Semester Final Presentation","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-06-18-maeumjigi-23"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #21] Optimizing Data Mix Ratios and Starting the 4th Fine-Tuning","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-22-maeumjigi-21"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nIn the final week of May (27th-30th), we dedicated our efforts to wrapping up the results of the highly anticipated **4th Fine-Tuning (fine_tuning_4)** and solidifying the system\'s framework.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Final Checkpoint 4400 and Graph Analysis\\r\\n\\r\\n> \\"Qwen35-9B_mixed_7_3/checkpoints/checkpoint-4400/adapter_config.json\\"\\r\\n\\r\\nThe 4th fine-tuning model, trained with a mix of daily casual chat and emotional counseling data, finally finished training and yielded the optimal weight: `checkpoint-4400`.\\r\\n\\r\\nIn previous fine-tunings, there was an overfitting issue where the model would respond in an overly serious counseling mode even to casual chat. As a result of experimenting with the data mix ratio, the model has finally been reborn as a \'perceptive\' chatbot that perfectly distinguishes between and appropriately responds to daily conversation and serious crisis counseling!\\r\\n\\r\\nWe visualized all evaluation metrics with `matplotlib` graphs to prove the performance differences at a glance.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Chatbot System Architecture Design and Final Report\\r\\n\\r\\n> \\"chatbot_architecture.html generation complete\\"\\r\\n> \\"Please write the results of fine_tuning_4 as a report (.docx).\\"\\r\\n\\r\\n![System Architecture](./images/02_architecture.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nTo serve the verified model as a real app, we designed the entire system architecture and Knowledge Graph integration plan under the name `chatbot_architecture.html`.\\r\\n\\r\\nAnd on May 30th, with the Artificial Intelligence (AI)\'s help, we automatically generated the `fine_tuning_4_report.docx`, successfully concluding Phase 1 of our fine-tuning project. The report bluntly contains the actual response text comparisons between the original base model and our fine-tuned model, proving that all our hard work was not in vain!\\r\\n\\r\\nWith this, the breathless May dev logs of the Maeumjigi project are all wrapped up. What new challenges await in June?"},{"id":"2026-05-22-maeumjigi-21","metadata":{"permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-22-maeumjigi-21","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2026-05-22-maeumjigi-21/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-22-maeumjigi-21/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #21] Optimizing Data Mix Ratios and Starting the 4th Fine-Tuning","description":"Cover Image","date":"2026-05-22T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"fine-tuning","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/fine-tuning"},{"inline":true,"label":"dataset","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/dataset"},{"inline":true,"label":"prompt","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/prompt"},{"inline":true,"label":"capstone","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/capstone"}],"readingTime":1.56,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"jaybee","title":"Maeumjigi Developer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-05-22-maeumjigi-21","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #21] Optimizing Data Mix Ratios and Starting the 4th Fine-Tuning","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","fine-tuning","dataset","prompt","capstone"],"date":"2026-05-22T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #22] 4th Fine-Tuning Complete! Architecture Design & Final Report","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-30-maeumjigi-22"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #20] 3rd Fine-Tuning Complete & Backend Feature Enhancements","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-20-maeumjigi-20"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nOn May 21st and 22nd, the Maeumjigi Capstone team embarked on massive data generation and preparation for the **4th Fine-Tuning (fine_tuning_4)**.\\r\\n\\r\\n### The Tug-of-War Between Emotional and Daily Conversations\\r\\n\\r\\nWhile testing the 3rd fine-tuning model, I discovered a critical issue.\\r\\n\\r\\n> \\"When fine-tuning with the haiku_pred dataset, the responses seem too heavily skewed towards emotional counseling. How should we fine-tune to maintain the original model\'s daily conversation abilities?\\"\\r\\n\\r\\nOur goal is a \'suicide prevention counselor conveying warm empathy\', but the model was showing signs of **overfitting**, giving overly serious and emotional responses even to mundane questions like \\"How\'s the weather?\\" or \\"What should I eat?\\".\\r\\n\\r\\nTo solve this, we planned an experiment to train the model by mixing emotional data and daily chat data at appropriate ratios.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Generating 10,000 v6 Emotion Dataset Entries via Application Programming Interface (API)\\r\\n\\r\\n> \\"Unify to 512 tokens / Average 500 tokens per entry / 20 Personas / File: v6.jsonl\\"\\r\\n\\r\\nTo create the best dataset, I used the Anthropic API (Haiku) Batch mode to generate a staggering 10,000 entries of emotional conversation data (`Emotion_train_dataset_v6.jsonl`). Though there was a frustrating moment when the API batch ran in the background without showing progress, I filled in the missing entries with an additional script (`fill_v6.py`) to complete the 10,000 count.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Finding the Golden Ratio (fine_tuning_4)\\r\\n\\r\\n> \\"Emotion_train_dataset_v6.jsonl is complete. Now make a 7:3 ratio dataset, a 2:1 ratio, and a 5:5 ratio dataset.\\"\\r\\n> \\"This 4th fine-tuning will be based on the training dataset mix ratio (mixed_2_1, mixed_5_5, mixed_7_3) instead of alpha values.\\"\\r\\n\\r\\n![Ratio Experiment](./images/02_ratio.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nThe 4th fine-tuning has now become an arena to verify which data ratio perfectly harmonizes the Artificial Intelligence (AI)\'s \'daily casualness\' with \'professional counseling\'. We generated three versions of the dataset and started injecting them into the Qwen3.5-9B model!"},{"id":"2026-05-20-maeumjigi-20","metadata":{"permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-20-maeumjigi-20","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2026-05-20-maeumjigi-20/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-20-maeumjigi-20/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #20] 3rd Fine-Tuning Complete & Backend Feature Enhancements","description":"Cover Image","date":"2026-05-20T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"fine-tuning","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/fine-tuning"},{"inline":true,"label":"backend","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/backend"},{"inline":true,"label":"frontend","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/frontend"},{"inline":true,"label":"database","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/database"},{"inline":true,"label":"capstone","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/capstone"}],"readingTime":1.87,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"jaybee","title":"Maeumjigi Developer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-05-20-maeumjigi-20","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #20] 3rd Fine-Tuning Complete & Backend Feature Enhancements","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","fine-tuning","backend","frontend","database","capstone"],"date":"2026-05-20T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #21] Optimizing Data Mix Ratios and Starting the 4th Fine-Tuning","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-22-maeumjigi-21"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Webots Robotics] Deterministic Evaluation via evaluate_qlearning & Benchmark vs. DQN and SARSA (#4)","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-20-rl-qlearning-4"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nOver the two days of May 19th and 20th, the automation of evaluation for the 3rd fine-tuning (`fine_tuning_3`) and the enhancement of backend/frontend features for building the actual app service proceeded in parallel.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Concluding the 3rd Fine-Tuning and Automation Scripts\\r\\n\\r\\nBased on the insights gained from the previous fine-tuning, the training for the 3rd fine-tuning, which was set up with concepts of knowledge graphs and ontologies in mind, was completed.\\r\\n\\r\\n> \\"Select the checkpoint with the Best Eval from the experimental models in the fine_tuning_3 folder, replace the files in the adapter folder, and report the results.\\"\\r\\n> \\"Write a run_test_20_30.sh file that runs inference and evaluation in parallel for fine_tuning_3 as well.\\"\\r\\n\\r\\nInstead of manually digging through folders to copy checkpoints like before, I instructed the Artificial Intelligence (AI) to write a script that automatically extracted the best performing model weights (Best Checkpoint) and set them up in the `adapter` folder. Then, I executed the parallel inference script, `run_test_20_30.sh`, throwing the evaluation for the 4 Alias models to the background server.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Backend/Frontend: Chatbot Database (DB) Saving and Community Board Features\\r\\n\\r\\nWhile the models were hard at work running inferences on the server, I accelerated the `mental-health` DB and app integration work in my local environment.\\r\\n\\r\\n> \\"Summarize seed_data.sql. What is the pw for users? Is it Test1234!? What was the db I was trying to apply this sql to?\\"\\r\\n\\r\\nTo break through the login issue that caused the frustrating `401 Unauthorized` error last time, I thoroughly examined `seed_data.sql` to verify the password hashing and user data.\\r\\n\\r\\nAs a result, I was able to leave a meaningful Git commit on the afternoon of May 20th:\\r\\n`feat: Added chatbot DB save, board pagination, and emotion diary filter`\\r\\n\\r\\n![App Features](./images/02_app_feature.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nFinally, the conversation logs of our fine-tuned Large Language Model (LLM) chatbot officially started being saved to the DB! In addition, the pagination feature for the community board where users can communicate, and the emotion diary filter feature for recording daily moods were successfully added to the frontend.\\r\\n\\r\\nIt was a thrilling moment when the massive, invisible fine-tuned model finally began to be served in the beautiful vessel of a mobile app."},{"id":"2026-05-20-rl-qlearning-4","metadata":{"permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-20-rl-qlearning-4","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2026-05-20-rl-qlearning-4/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-20-rl-qlearning-4/index.md","title":"[Webots Robotics] Deterministic Evaluation via evaluate_qlearning & Benchmark vs. DQN and SARSA (#4)","description":"Zero-exploration (\u03B5=0) deterministic evaluation pipeline with evaluate_qlearning.py, examining path efficiency, near-miss metrics, and benchmark comparisons against DQN and SARSA.","date":"2026-05-20T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"reinforcement-learning","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/reinforcement-learning"},{"inline":true,"label":"q-learning","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/q-learning"},{"inline":true,"label":"evaluation","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/evaluation"},{"inline":true,"label":"benchmark","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/benchmark"},{"inline":true,"label":"dqn","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/dqn"},{"inline":true,"label":"sarsa","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/sarsa"},{"inline":true,"label":"robotics","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/robotics"},{"inline":true,"label":"python","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/python"}],"readingTime":3.17,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"jaybee","title":"Maeumjigi Developer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-05-20-rl-qlearning-4","title":"[Webots Robotics] Deterministic Evaluation via evaluate_qlearning & Benchmark vs. DQN and SARSA (#4)","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["reinforcement-learning","q-learning","evaluation","benchmark","dqn","sarsa","robotics","python"],"date":"2026-05-20T00:00:00.000Z","project":"rl_q_learning","description":"Zero-exploration (\u03B5=0) deterministic evaluation pipeline with evaluate_qlearning.py, examining path efficiency, near-miss metrics, and benchmark comparisons against DQN and SARSA."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #20] 3rd Fine-Tuning Complete & Backend Feature Enhancements","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-20-maeumjigi-20"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Webots Robotics] Conquering Local Minima in 12-Wall Obstacles: Multi-Seed Headless Simulation (#3)","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-19-rl-qlearning-3"}},"content":"![Success and Steps Benchmark](./images/01_eval_success_steps.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n> \u{1F4CC} **Key Takeaways (TL;DR)**\\r\\n> - **Objective**: Measure the pure, deterministic performance of the learned greedy policy (\u03B5=0) without exploration noise, validating real-world deployability.\\r\\n> - **Methodology**: Implemented `evaluate_qlearning.py` running 30 independent trials across 5 round-robin evaluation seeds, rigorously tracking success rates, path efficiency vs. theoretical shortest routes, and obstacle near-miss counts.\\r\\n> - **Result**: Tabular Q-Learning attained a 93.3% deterministic success rate in the 12-wall arena with superior path smoothness and ultra-low 0.02ms inference latency, outperforming heavier DQN and SARSA baselines.\\r\\n\\r\\n{/* truncate */}\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 1. Quantitative Evaluation Pipeline (`evaluate_qlearning.py`)\\r\\n\\r\\nTraining reward curves are inherently noisy due to $\\\\epsilon$-greedy exploration. To evaluate the true deployment capability of our agent, we must test the **purely deterministic policy ($\\\\epsilon = 0$)**.\\r\\n\\r\\nWe built an evaluation runner that loads the saved Q-table (`qlearning_qtable.npy`) and evaluates the agent across 5 distinct test seeds (`EVAL_SEEDS = (0, 1, 2, 3, 4)`).\\r\\n\\r\\n```mermaid\\r\\nflowchart LR\\r\\n    Model[\\"Trained Q-Table<br>(qlearning_qtable.npy)\\"] --\x3e Agent[\\"Greedy Agent<br>(\u03B5 = 0 Fixed)\\"]\\r\\n    EvalSeeds[\\"5 Test Seeds<br>(EVAL_SEEDS 0..4)\\"] --\x3e Webots[\\"Webots Simulator<br>(30 Evaluation Trials)\\"]\\r\\n    Agent <--\x3e Webots\\r\\n    Webots --\x3e CSV[\\"Trace & Metric Logging<br>(eval_log.csv / traces)\\"]\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n### \u{1F4BB} Evaluation Loop Architecture\\r\\n\\r\\n```python\\r\\n# evaluate_qlearning.py excerpt\\r\\nagent = QLearningAgent(seed=0)\\r\\nagent.load(model_path) # Load trained Q-Table\\r\\n\\r\\nfor ep in range(n_episodes):\\r\\n    env_seed = EVAL_SEEDS[ep % len(EVAL_SEEDS)]\\r\\n    raw, info = env.reset(seed=env_seed, return_info=True)\\r\\n    s = discretize(raw)\\r\\n    \\r\\n    for t in range(1, max_steps + 1):\\r\\n        a = agent.act(s, deterministic=True) # Pure argmax Q(s, a)\\r\\n        raw_next, r, done, info = env.step(a)\\r\\n        s = discretize(raw_next)\\r\\n        \\r\\n        # Log trajectory coordinates and near-misses (dist <= 0.15m)\\r\\n        record_metrics(info)\\r\\n        if done: break\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 2. Comprehensive Evaluation Metrics\\r\\n\\r\\n1. **Success Rate**: Percentage of runs reaching the target radius (0.10m) within 150 steps without collision.\\r\\n2. **Path Efficiency**: Ratio of theoretical shortest path distance (L_shortest) to actual traversed path length (L_actual).\\r\\n   **Path Efficiency (%) = (Shortest Path / Actual Path) \xd7 100%**\\r\\n3. **Near-Miss Frequency**: Number of steps where obstacle distance dipped below 0.15m.\\r\\n4. **Oscillation Count**: Metric penalizing back-and-forth steering jitter.\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 3. Algorithm Benchmark: Tabular Q-Learning vs. SARSA vs. DQN\\r\\n\\r\\n![Trajectory Quality & Benchmarks](./images/02_eval_quality.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nComparative evaluation in the identical Webots 12-wall arena across the three reinforcement learning algorithms revealed clear performance trade-offs:\\r\\n\\r\\n| Metric | Tabular Q-Learning (Ours) | SARSA (On-policy) | Deep Q-Network (DQN) |\\r\\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: |\\r\\n| **Learning Paradigm** | Off-policy TD(0) | On-policy TD(0) | Neural Network (Deep RL) |\\r\\n| **Parameter Size** | **2,592 floats (~2.5 KB)** | 2,592 floats (~2.5 KB) | ~150,000 weights (~600 KB) |\\r\\n| **Deterministic Success Rate (`obstacle12`)** | **93.3%** | 86.7% | 90.0% |\\r\\n| **Average Steps per Episode** | **41.2 steps** | 48.5 steps | 44.7 steps |\\r\\n| **Per-Step Inference Latency** | **0.02 ms** (NumPy array lookup) | 0.02 ms | 2.15 ms (PyTorch forward pass) |\\r\\n| **Near-Miss Frequency** | Low (balanced detour) | Minimal (excessively conservative) | Moderate |\\r\\n\\r\\n### \u{1F50D} In-Depth Behavioral Analysis\\r\\n* **Why Q-Learning Excels**: By evaluating `max_a\' Q(s\', a\')`, off-policy Q-Learning ignores transient exploratory collisions during training, settling on the **shortest feasible detour path**.\\r\\n* **SARSA\'s Conservative Bias**: On-policy updates factor in exploration penalties, causing SARSA to over-correct and choose excessively long, inefficient detours to avoid walls at all costs.\\r\\n* **DQN\'s Trade-off**: DQN handles continuous inputs well, but training stability requires replay buffers and target networks, while its 2.15ms latency and GPU requirement impose severe constraints on lightweight microcontrollers like Arduino.\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 4. Evaluation Takeaways\\r\\n\\r\\nOur discrete 648-bin **Tabular Q-Learning model requires just 2.5 KB of memory**, yet matched and exceeded deep neural network models in success rate (93.3%) and real-time execution speed.\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 5. What\'s Next?\\r\\n\\r\\nWith simulation benchmarks thoroughly concluded, the ultimate milestone remains: **transferring policy to reality (Sim-to-Real)**.\\r\\n\\r\\nIn our final Episode #5, we will detail the hardware integration: deploying our policy to a **2-wheel differential Arduino Uno robot, communicating via HC-06 Bluetooth, and tracking ground-truth position via ceiling webcam checkerboard calibration and ArUco markers**!"},{"id":"2026-05-19-rl-qlearning-3","metadata":{"permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-19-rl-qlearning-3","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2026-05-19-rl-qlearning-3/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-19-rl-qlearning-3/index.md","title":"[Webots Robotics] Conquering Local Minima in 12-Wall Obstacles: Multi-Seed Headless Simulation (#3)","description":"Overcoming the Local Minima dilemma in complex 12-wall obstacle environments (obstacle12) and orchestrating high-speed automated multi-seed Webots simulations.","date":"2026-05-19T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"reinforcement-learning","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/reinforcement-learning"},{"inline":true,"label":"q-learning","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/q-learning"},{"inline":true,"label":"webots","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/webots"},{"inline":true,"label":"obstacle-avoidance","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/obstacle-avoidance"},{"inline":true,"label":"local-minima","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/local-minima"},{"inline":true,"label":"headless","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/headless"},{"inline":true,"label":"python","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/python"}],"readingTime":3.08,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"jaybee","title":"Maeumjigi Developer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-05-19-rl-qlearning-3","title":"[Webots Robotics] Conquering Local Minima in 12-Wall Obstacles: Multi-Seed Headless Simulation (#3)","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["reinforcement-learning","q-learning","webots","obstacle-avoidance","local-minima","headless","python"],"date":"2026-05-19T00:00:00.000Z","project":"rl_q_learning","description":"Overcoming the Local Minima dilemma in complex 12-wall obstacle environments (obstacle12) and orchestrating high-speed automated multi-seed Webots simulations."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Webots Robotics] Deterministic Evaluation via evaluate_qlearning & Benchmark vs. DQN and SARSA (#4)","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-20-rl-qlearning-4"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Webots Robotics] Tabular Q-Learning Implementation & Reward Shaping Tuning (#2)","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-18-rl-qlearning-2"}},"content":"![obstacle12 Simulation Arena](./images/01_obstacle12_arena.png)\\n\\n> \u{1F4CC} **Key Takeaways (TL;DR)**\\n> - **Problem**: When a goal lies directly behind an obstacle, the myopic nature of distance-delta rewards (+0.1 \xd7 \u0394dist) pulls the robot into head-on collisions, trapping it in severe **Local Minima**.\\n> - **Solution**: Integrated 3-way head-scan obstacle perception (front/left/right) with long-horizon Bellman value propagation (\u03B3=0.99), alongside an automated PowerShell **Webots Headless pipeline (`headless_run.ps1`)** executing multi-seed training across seeds 0, 1, and 2.\\n> - **Result**: The agent successfully learned robust detour policies in the 12-wall arena, achieving a 90%+ goal success rate after 600 episodes.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. The Obstacle Challenge: The Local Minima Trap\\n\\nIn an empty arena (`no_obstacle`), navigating is simply a matter of steering toward the target heading. However, introducing **12 interior walls (`obstacle12`)** unveiled a critical bottleneck in reward design:\\n\\n```mermaid\\nflowchart TD\\n    Start[\\"Start (Robot)\\"]\\n    Wall[\\"Obstacle Wall\\"]\\n    Goal[\\"Target Goal\\"]\\n    \\n    Start --\x3e|\\"Direct drive (greedy distance reward)\\"| Wall\\n    Wall -.->|\\"Collision & deadlocked (Local Minima)\\"| Wall\\n    Start ==>|\\"Detour Policy<br>Accepts temporary distance penalty\\"| Goal\\n```\\n\\n* **The Dilemma**: When the target is directly on the other side of an interior wall, driving straight ahead leads directly to collision.\\n* **Reward Conflict**: Circumnavigating the wall requires moving temporarily away from the goal (negative distance delta). Under naive exploration, the agent enters a deadlock\u2014spinning or timing out against the wall.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Solution Strategy: Head-Scan Perception & Value Backpropagation\\n\\nOvercoming this trap relies on coupling **3-directional head-scan perception** with a **high discount factor ($\\\\gamma = 0.99$)**:\\n\\n1. **Perceptual State Discretization**:\\n   * Attempting to advance while `front_distance = Danger` yields a heavy collision penalty (-1.0).\\n   * Sweeping the sensor head left and right reveals free avenues (`left_distance = Safe` or `right_distance = Safe`), encouraging lateral turns.\\n2. **Long-Term Temporal Difference Credit Assignment**:\\n   * Although turning away temporarily depresses the step reward, successfully rounding the wall and reaching the target yields the substantial terminal reward (+1.0).\\n   * Backpropagated through $Q(s, a)$ with $\\\\gamma = 0.99$, the agent gradually learns that detour steps possess higher expected return than head-on approaches.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Webots Headless Automation Pipeline\\n\\nRigorous reinforcement learning requires **multi-seed validation (Seeds 0, 1, 2)**. Running 1,500 episodes interactively in the Webots GUI three times would take many hours.\\n\\nWe automated this with a **`headless_run.ps1` PowerShell script**, launching Webots in high-speed, no-render mode (`--mode=fast --no-rendering`):\\n\\n### \u{1F4BB} Automated Pipeline (`headless_run.ps1`)\\n\\n```powershell\\n# Launch Webots in background headless fast mode\\n$webotsProc = Start-Process -FilePath $WebotsExe `\\n    -ArgumentList @(\\"--mode=fast\\", \\"--no-rendering\\", \\"--stdout\\", \\"--stderr\\", $WorldFile) `\\n    -PassThru\\n\\n# Train agent over socket IPC\\npython train_qlearning.py --scenario obstacle12 --seed 0 --episodes 1500\\n\\n# Cleanup process and generate performance plots\\nStop-Process -Id $webotsProc.Id -Force\\npython -m qlearning.plot_curves --log-dir logs/obstacle12/seed_0\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Empirical Evaluation: Cross-Environment Learning Curves\\n\\n![Cross-Environment Learning Curves](./images/02_comparison_curves.png)\\n\\nThe graph above contrasts convergence across 3 random seeds between the unobstructed world (`no_obstacle`) and the 12-wall obstacle world (`obstacle12`).\\n\\n### \u{1F4CA} Benchmark Metrics\\n\\n| Metric | Empty Arena (`no_obstacle`) | 12-Wall Arena (`obstacle12`) |\\n| :--- | :---: | :---: |\\n| **Initial Convergence** | ~300 - 400 episodes | ~600 - 800 episodes |\\n| **Final Success Rate (100-ep MA)** | **98.5% - 99.2%** | **91.4% - 94.8%** |\\n| **Mean Steps per Episode** | 15 - 22 steps | 38 - 52 steps (detour path) |\\n| **Cross-Seed Variance** | Negligible (highly stable) | Moderate (exploration path dependency) |\\n\\n* **Extended Exploration**: In `obstacle12`, discovering viable detour corridors requires approximately 200 additional episodes of random trials, keeping the average reward depressed until episode 400.\\n* **Detour Mastery**: Beyond episode 800, all seeds stabilize above 90% success, confirming that the agent reliably maneuvers through tight gaps without collisions.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. What\'s Next?\\n\\nOur 648-bin Tabular Q-Learning model successfully navigates maze-like obstacle configurations.\\n\\nIn Episode #4, we will load the saved model checkpoints (`models/*.npy`) to benchmark greedy, deterministic performance using **`evaluate_qlearning.py` and analyze comparative metrics against DQN and SARSA**!"},{"id":"2026-05-18-rl-qlearning-2","metadata":{"permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-18-rl-qlearning-2","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2026-05-18-rl-qlearning-2/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-18-rl-qlearning-2/index.md","title":"[Webots Robotics] Tabular Q-Learning Implementation & Reward Shaping Tuning (#2)","description":"In-depth breakdown of Tabular Q-Learning TD(0) update formulas, exponential epsilon-decay exploration, and dense reward shaping to overcome sparse rewards in mobile robot navigation.","date":"2026-05-18T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"reinforcement-learning","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/reinforcement-learning"},{"inline":true,"label":"q-learning","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/q-learning"},{"inline":true,"label":"reward-shaping","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/reward-shaping"},{"inline":true,"label":"webots","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/webots"},{"inline":true,"label":"robotics","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/robotics"},{"inline":true,"label":"machine-learning","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/machine-learning"},{"inline":true,"label":"python","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/python"}],"readingTime":4.09,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"jaybee","title":"Maeumjigi Developer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-05-18-rl-qlearning-2","title":"[Webots Robotics] Tabular Q-Learning Implementation & Reward Shaping Tuning (#2)","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["reinforcement-learning","q-learning","reward-shaping","webots","robotics","machine-learning","python"],"date":"2026-05-18T00:00:00.000Z","project":"rl_q_learning","description":"In-depth breakdown of Tabular Q-Learning TD(0) update formulas, exponential epsilon-decay exploration, and dense reward shaping to overcome sparse rewards in mobile robot navigation."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Webots Robotics] Conquering Local Minima in 12-Wall Obstacles: Multi-Seed Headless Simulation (#3)","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-19-rl-qlearning-3"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #19] Automating Reports and Backend Database (DB) Troubleshooting","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-17-maeumjigi-19"}},"content":"![Training Reward Curve](./images/01_reward_curve.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n> \u{1F4CC} **Key Takeaways (TL;DR)**\\r\\n> - **Problem**: Sparse reward setups (+1.0 at goal, -1.0 at collision) cause severe exploration delays, causing the agent to wander or spin for thousands of episodes before stumbling upon the target.\\r\\n> - **Solution**: Implemented a multi-objective **Dense Reward Shaping** mechanism combining potential-based Euclidean distance delta rewards (+0.1 \xd7 \u0394dist) with step (-0.01), turning (-0.01), and backward (-0.05) penalties, paired with exponential epsilon decay (1.0 \u2192 0.05).\\r\\n> - **Result**: The moving average reward shifted into a steady positive regime (0.7+) within 500 episodes in the `no_obstacle` scenario, achieving a 98%+ goal success rate.\\r\\n\\r\\n{/* truncate */}\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 1. Tabular Q-Learning Update Formula & Agent Architecture\\r\\n\\r\\nTabular Q-Learning is an **off-policy Temporal Difference (TD(0))** algorithm grounded in the Bellman Optimality Equation.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe agent maintains an action-value table $Q(s, a)$ structured as a 2D NumPy array of shape $(648, 4)$. Using the experience transition tuple $(s, a, r, s\', done)$ streamed from the Webots socket server, the $Q$-values are updated in real time via:\\r\\n\\r\\n### \u{1F4D0} Q-Learning TD(0) Update Rule\\r\\n\\r\\n```text\\r\\nTarget = r + \u03B3 * max_a\' Q(s\', a\') * (1 - done)\\r\\nTD Error = Target - Q(s, a)\\r\\nQ(s, a) <- Q(s, a) + \u03B1 * TD Error\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n* **Learning Rate ($\\\\alpha = 0.1$)**: Balances the rate of incorporating new feedback versus retaining historical estimates.\\r\\n* **Discount Factor ($\\\\gamma = 0.99$)**: Values long-term future returns, critical for multi-step navigation.\\r\\n* **Terminal Masking (`1 - done`)**: Clamps future expectations to zero upon goal arrival or collision, isolating the terminal reward.\\r\\n\\r\\n### \u{1F4BB} Agent Core Implementation (`qlearning_agent.py`)\\r\\n\\r\\n```python\\r\\nimport numpy as np\\r\\n\\r\\nclass QLearningAgent:\\r\\n    def __init__(self, n_states=648, n_actions=4, alpha=0.1, gamma=0.99,\\r\\n                 eps_start=1.0, eps_min=0.05, eps_decay=0.995, seed=0):\\r\\n        self.rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\\r\\n        self.Q = np.zeros((n_states, n_actions), dtype=np.float64)\\r\\n        self.alpha = float(alpha)\\r\\n        self.gamma = float(gamma)\\r\\n        self.epsilon = float(eps_start)\\r\\n        self.eps_min = float(eps_min)\\r\\n        self.eps_decay = float(eps_decay)\\r\\n        self.n_actions = int(n_actions)\\r\\n\\r\\n    def act(self, s: int, deterministic: bool = False) -> int:\\r\\n        \\"\\"\\"\u03B5-Greedy behavior policy: Exploration vs. Exploitation\\"\\"\\"\\r\\n        if (not deterministic) and self.rng.random() < self.epsilon:\\r\\n            return int(self.rng.integers(self.n_actions))\\r\\n        return int(np.argmax(self.Q[s]))\\r\\n\\r\\n    def update(self, s: int, a: int, r: float, s_next: int, done: bool) -> float:\\r\\n        \\"\\"\\"TD(0) Off-policy Q-Table update\\"\\"\\"\\r\\n        target = r if done else r + self.gamma * float(np.max(self.Q[s_next]))\\r\\n        td_error = target - self.Q[s, a]\\r\\n        self.Q[s, a] += self.alpha * td_error\\r\\n        return float(td_error)\\r\\n\\r\\n    def decay_epsilon(self):\\r\\n        \\"\\"\\"Exponential decay executed at the end of each episode\\"\\"\\"\\r\\n        self.epsilon = max(self.eps_min, self.epsilon * self.eps_decay)\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 2. Exponential Epsilon-Greedy Decay Strategy\\r\\n\\r\\nAt the start of training, the agent has zero domain knowledge, requiring **$\\\\epsilon = 1.0$ (100% random exploration)** to discover the arena boundaries.\\r\\n\\r\\nHowever, an uncurbed exploration rate induces perpetual collisions even after the optimal path has been resolved. We apply per-episode exponential decay:\\r\\n\\r\\n**\u03B5_(ep+1) = max(\u03B5_min, \u03B5_ep \xd7 0.995)**\\r\\n\\r\\n* **Episode 1**: $\\\\epsilon = 1.0$ (Full exploration)\\r\\n* **Episode 300**: $\\\\epsilon \\\\approx 0.22$ (Shift toward exploiting high Q-values)\\r\\n* **Episode 600**: $\\\\epsilon = 0.05$ (Floor exploration rate $\\\\epsilon__min$, 95% greedy execution)\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 3. Dense Reward Shaping for Sparse Navigation\\r\\n\\r\\nA fundamental challenge in mobile robotics is **sparse reward feedback**. In a $1.2m \\\\times 1.2m$ arena, moving randomly yields zero reward until the robot coincidentally intersects the $0.10m$ goal tolerance.\\r\\n\\r\\nTo guide convergence, we engineered a **Dense Reward Shaping** schedule inside the Webots controller (`RL_controller.py`):\\r\\n\\r\\n| Reward Component | Value | Design Objective & Physical Effect |\\r\\n| :--- | :---: | :--- |\\r\\n| **Goal Reached (`REWARD_GOAL`)** | **+1.0** | Terminal reward on successful target arrival |\\r\\n| **Wall/Obstacle Collision (`REWARD_COLLISION`)** | **-1.0** | Severe penalty on physical impact |\\r\\n| **Distance Delta Reward (`REWARD_DELTA_SCALE`)** | **+0.1 \xd7 \u0394dist** | Immediate feedback proportional to Euclidean distance reduction |\\r\\n| **Step Time Penalty (`REWARD_STEP`)** | **-0.01** | Prevents idling and incentivizes shortest paths |\\r\\n| **Turning Penalty (`REWARD_TURN`)** | **-0.01** | Suppresses unproductive spinning behavior |\\r\\n| **Backward Penalty (`REWARD_BACKWARD`)** | **-0.05** | Discourages inefficient reverse driving |\\r\\n\\r\\n### \u{1F50D} Mathematical Principle of Distance Delta\\r\\n**\u0394dist = dist_prev - dist_curr**\\r\\n* Driving $0.05m$ toward the target awards $+0.1 \\\\times 0.05 = +0.005$ immediately.\\r\\n* Conversely, driving away incurs an instant penalty, allowing the agent to align its heading with the target within the first 100 episodes.\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 4. Empirical Evaluation: Reward and Success Curves\\r\\n\\r\\nTraining for 1,500 episodes in the `no_obstacle` world demonstrated remarkable convergence matching our theoretical expectations.\\r\\n\\r\\n![Success Rate Curve](./images/02_success_curve.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n### \u{1F4C8} Convergence Milestones\\r\\n1. **Episodes 0 ~ 200 (Exploration Phase)**:\\r\\n   * Frequent wall collisions and 150-step timeouts keep the moving average reward in the negative range (-0.4 ~ -0.2).\\r\\n2. **Episodes 200 ~ 500 (Ascent Phase)**:\\r\\n   * As distance-delta rewards propagate through the Q-Table, the policy establishes a strong forward-driving preference whenever `goal_heading` is centered.\\r\\n   * Success rate surges from 20% to **over 80%**.\\r\\n3. **Episodes 500 ~ 1,500 (Asymptotic Convergence)**:\\r\\n   * With $\\\\epsilon$ settled at 0.05, average steps per episode plummet from 150 (timeout) to **15 ~ 22 steps**.\\r\\n   * The 100-episode moving average success rate converges above **98.5%**.\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 5. What\'s Next?\\r\\n\\r\\nWe have proven that Tabular Q-Learning over 648 states solves empty arena navigation cleanly.\\r\\n\\r\\nHowever, **real-world environments are packed with walls and obstacles**. In Episode #3, we will investigate the **`obstacle12` and `obstacle_hard` maps**, examining how our agent handles local minima traps and running multi-seed parallel training!"},{"id":"2026-05-17-maeumjigi-19","metadata":{"permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-17-maeumjigi-19","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2026-05-17-maeumjigi-19/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-17-maeumjigi-19/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #19] Automating Reports and Backend Database (DB) Troubleshooting","description":"Cover Image","date":"2026-05-17T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"fine-tuning","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/fine-tuning"},{"inline":true,"label":"report","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/report"},{"inline":true,"label":"backend","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/backend"},{"inline":true,"label":"DB","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/db"},{"inline":true,"label":"troubleshooting","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/troubleshooting"},{"inline":true,"label":"capstone","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/capstone"}],"readingTime":1.45,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"jaybee","title":"Maeumjigi Developer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-05-17-maeumjigi-19","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #19] Automating Reports and Backend Database (DB) Troubleshooting","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","fine-tuning","report","backend","DB","troubleshooting","capstone"],"date":"2026-05-17T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Webots Robotics] Tabular Q-Learning Implementation & Reward Shaping Tuning (#2)","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-18-rl-qlearning-2"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Webots Robotics] Q-Learning Autonomous Navigation: 648-Bin State Discretization & Socket Architecture (#1)","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-17-rl-qlearning-1"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nI must correct my previous assumption that \\"May 13th was the last of Maeumjigi\'s May development\\"! On May 17th, a massive wave of backend integration testing and report drafting hit us.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Automating the Fine-Tuning Report using Artificial Intelligence (AI)\\r\\n\\r\\nThere was simply not enough time for a human to manually analyze the massive result graphs from the 2nd fine-tuning (`fine_tuning_2`).\\r\\n\\r\\n> \\"Create a prompt to write a report comparing the inference results of the models. Save it as report_prompt.md and write the report.\\"\\r\\n\\r\\nI attempted prompt engineering to have the AI interpret the metrics and automatically draft the report. Ultimately, the AI analyzed the pros and cons of each model and the differences based on Alpha values, generating an excellent Word (.docx) report draft.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Backend DB Integration and the Swamp of 401 Unauthorized\\r\\n\\r\\nRight after the report, I started pushing `backup.sql` data into the local PostgreSQL DB to test the system stability of the frontend and backend.\\r\\n\\r\\n> \\"I logged in successfully after running the backend and frontend, but nothing responds when I click menus.\\"\\r\\n> \\"Email or password incorrect... INFO: POST /auth/login 401 Unauthorized\\"\\r\\n\\r\\nI successfully pushed the DB dump and accessed the web, but fell into a hell of `401 Unauthorized` login errors. I created `debug_login.py` and endlessly restarted the uvicorn server for troubleshooting.\\r\\n\\r\\n![DB Troubleshooting](./images/02_db.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n### Contemplating Knowledge Graphs\\r\\n\\r\\n> \\"Techniques using ontology and knowledge graphs are rising. Using these might create a chatbot with better stability than just focusing on a single Large Language Model (LLM).\\"\\r\\n\\r\\nLate at night, to overcome the limitations of simple LLM fine-tuning, I began designing `chatbot_design_prompt.md` to explore fusing ontologies and Knowledge Graphs. As we pass mid-May, the scale of the project is growing massive!"},{"id":"2026-05-17-rl-qlearning-1","metadata":{"permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-17-rl-qlearning-1","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2026-05-17-rl-qlearning-1/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-17-rl-qlearning-1/index.md","title":"[Webots Robotics] Q-Learning Autonomous Navigation: 648-Bin State Discretization & Socket Architecture (#1)","description":"Designing a 648-bin state space discretizer and a TCP socket IPC architecture for Webots mobile robot autonomous navigation with tabular Q-Learning.","date":"2026-05-17T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"reinforcement-learning","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/reinforcement-learning"},{"inline":true,"label":"q-learning","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/q-learning"},{"inline":true,"label":"webots","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/webots"},{"inline":true,"label":"robotics","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/robotics"},{"inline":true,"label":"discretization","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/discretization"},{"inline":true,"label":"python","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/python"}],"readingTime":3.57,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"jaybee","title":"Maeumjigi Developer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-05-17-rl-qlearning-1","title":"[Webots Robotics] Q-Learning Autonomous Navigation: 648-Bin State Discretization & Socket Architecture (#1)","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["reinforcement-learning","q-learning","webots","robotics","discretization","python"],"date":"2026-05-17T00:00:00.000Z","project":"rl_q_learning","description":"Designing a 648-bin state space discretizer and a TCP socket IPC architecture for Webots mobile robot autonomous navigation with tabular Q-Learning."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #19] Automating Reports and Backend Database (DB) Troubleshooting","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-17-maeumjigi-19"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #18] Expanding Inference Tokens and Comparing 2nd Fine-Tuning Results","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-13-maeumjigi-18"}},"content":"![Webots Simulation Environment](./images/01_webots_arena.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n> \u{1F4CC} **Key Takeaways (TL;DR)**\\r\\n> - **Problem**: Continuous sensor readings (distance & heading) cause a state-space explosion for tabular Q-learning, and synchronous IPC with the Webots simulator requires low-latency coordination.\\r\\n> - **Solution**: Designed a geometrically partitioned 5-dimensional continuous sensor mapper producing 3 \xd7 8 \xd7 3 \xd7 3 \xd7 3 = 648 discrete states (`discretizer.py`) and a line-delimited JSON IPC socket pipeline over TCP port 5000.\\r\\n> - **Result**: Achieved $O(1)$ fast Q-Table indexing and reliable 32ms physics synchronization between the simulator and Python agent.\\r\\n\\r\\n{/* truncate */}\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 1. Project Background: Why Webots and Q-Learning?\\r\\n\\r\\nEnabling an autonomous differential-drive robot to navigate an unknown indoor arena and reach a target while avoiding obstacles is a foundational robotics challenge. While Deep Reinforcement Learning (DQN, PPO) handles complex inputs, it demands high computational overhead and lacks straightforward interpretability.\\r\\n\\r\\nIn this project, we apply **classical Tabular Q-Learning (Off-policy TD(0))** to a mobile robot in the Webots 3D physics engine. Our objective is to establish an optimal, mathematically verifiable navigation and obstacle avoidance policy in a 1.2m \xd7 1.2m indoor arena.\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 2. System Architecture: Webots Controller \u2194 Python RL Engine\\r\\n\\r\\nThe system is decoupled into two clean tiers: the **Webots simulation server** and the **Python RL client**.\\r\\n\\r\\n```mermaid\\r\\nflowchart LR\\r\\n    subgraph Webots[\\"Webots 3D Simulator (localhost:5000)\\"]\\r\\n        Robot[\\"Supervisor Robot<br>(Ultrasonic Sensor + Wheels)\\"]\\r\\n        HeadScan[\\"Head-scan Mechanism<br>(Left/Right 1.1 rad servo)\\"]\\r\\n        Server[\\"TCP Socket Server<br>(Line-delimited JSON)\\"]\\r\\n        Robot -\u2192 HeadScan -\u2192 Server\\r\\n    end\\r\\n\\r\\n    subgraph Python[\\"Python RL Engine (Client)\\"]\\r\\n        Env[\\"WebotsEnv (gym-like)<br>reset() / step()\\"]\\r\\n        Disc[\\"Discretizer<br>(Continuous \u2192 0~647 Bin)\\"]\\r\\n        Agent[\\"QLearningAgent<br>(Q-Table 648x4)\\"]\\r\\n        Env -\u2192 Disc -\u2192 Agent\\r\\n    end\\r\\n\\r\\n    Server <-\u2192|\\"request: {type: step, action: 0..3}<br>response: {state: [...], reward, done}\\"| Env\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n* **Webots Controller (`RL_controller.py`)**: Executes action commands for 480ms (15 simulation ticks $\xd7$ 32ms) and rotates a head-mounted ultrasonic sensor left and right by 1.1 rad ($\\\\approx 63\xb0$) to eliminate blind spots.\\r\\n* **Socket Protocol**: Lightweight line-delimited JSON over TCP localhost:5000:\\r\\n  * Request: `{\\"type\\": \\"step\\", \\"action\\": 0}` (0: Forward, 1: Backward, 2: Turn Left, 3: Turn Right)\\r\\n  * Response: `{\\"state\\": [0.45, 0.12, 0.35, 0.28, 0.40], \\"reward\\": 0.05, \\"done\\": false, \\"info\\": {...}}`\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 3. Discretizing Continuous Sensors into 648 States\\r\\n\\r\\nTo apply tabular Q-learning, continuous floats must be mapped into finite discrete states. We selected 5 crucial observation variables based on kinematic relevance:\\r\\n\\r\\n| Feature | Bins | Thresholds | Semantic Meaning |\\r\\n| :--- | :---: | :--- | :--- |\\r\\n| **`goal_distance`** | **3** | `[0.6m, 1.2m]` | Near, Mid, Far |\\r\\n| **`goal_heading`** | **8** | $[-\u03C0, \u03C0]$ (8 sectors, $45\xb0$ each) | Front, Front-Right, Right, Back-Right, Back, Back-Left, Left, Front-Left |\\r\\n| **`front_distance`** | **3** | `[0.15m, 0.30m]` | Danger/Collision, Caution, Safe |\\r\\n| **`left_distance`** | **3** | `[0.15m, 0.30m]` | Danger, Caution, Safe |\\r\\n| **`right_distance`** | **3** | `[0.15m, 0.30m]` | Danger, Caution, Safe |\\r\\n\\r\\nTotal State Count:\\r\\n**N_states = 3 \xd7 8 \xd7 3 \xd7 3 \xd7 3 = 648**\\r\\n\\r\\nThe resulting Q-Table requires only **$648 \xd7 4 = 2,592$ floating-point entries**, allowing sub-millisecond memory updates and zero GPU overhead.\\r\\n\\r\\n### \u{1F4BB} State Discretization Code (`discretizer.py`)\\r\\n\\r\\n```python\\r\\nimport numpy as np\\r\\n\\r\\n# Dimension boundaries\\r\\nN_GOAL_D, N_GOAL_H, N_FRONT, N_LEFT, N_RIGHT = 3, 8, 3, 3, 3\\r\\nN_STATES = N_GOAL_D * N_GOAL_H * N_FRONT * N_LEFT * N_RIGHT  # 648\\r\\n\\r\\n_HEADING_BINS = np.linspace(-np.pi, np.pi, 9)[1:-1]\\r\\n_NEAR_BINS    = np.array([0.15, 0.30])\\r\\n_DIST_BINS    = np.array([0.6, 1.2])\\r\\n\\r\\ndef discretize(state_dict: dict) \u2192 int:\\r\\n    \\"\\"\\"Maps 5-tuple continuous sensor readings to an integer in [0, 647].\\"\\"\\"\\r\\n    gd = int(np.digitize(state_dict[\\"goal_distance\\"], _DIST_BINS))\\r\\n    gh = int(np.digitize(state_dict[\\"goal_heading\\"], _HEADING_BINS))\\r\\n    df = int(np.digitize(state_dict[\\"front_distance\\"], _NEAR_BINS))\\r\\n    dl = int(np.digitize(state_dict[\\"left_distance\\"], _NEAR_BINS))\\r\\n    dr = int(np.digitize(state_dict[\\"right_distance\\"], _NEAR_BINS))\\r\\n\\r\\n    # Compute flattened 1D index\\r\\n    index = ((((gd * N_GOAL_H + gh) * N_FRONT + df) * N_LEFT + dl) * N_RIGHT + dr)\\r\\n    return int(index)\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 4. Head-Scan Mechanism & Collision Logic\\r\\n\\r\\n![System Architecture](./images/02_architecture.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nA fixed forward-facing sensor leaves lateral blind spots. We implemented an active **Head-Scan routine**:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. **Step Execution**: Drive motors at 3.0 rad/s for 480ms.\\r\\n2. **Head Rotation**: Sweep head servo +1.1 rad to read left distance, then -1.1 rad for right distance.\\r\\n3. **Re-center**: Return head to 0 rad and refresh front distance.\\r\\n4. **Terminal Conditions**:\\r\\n   * **Goal Reached**: Euclidean distance $`<= 0.10m` \\\\rightarrow$ success (`done = True`, reward $+1.0$)\\r\\n   * **Collision**: Any distance $`<= 0.08m` \\\\rightarrow$ failure (`done = True`, reward $-1.0$)\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 5. What\'s Next?\\r\\n\\r\\nWith the simulator environment, TCP socket IPC, and 648-bin state discretization established, our agent is ready for reinforcement learning.\\r\\n\\r\\nIn Episode #2, we will cover the **mathematical implementation of Tabular Q-Learning, $\\\\epsilon$-greedy exploration decay, and reward function tuning**!"},{"id":"2026-05-13-maeumjigi-18","metadata":{"permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-13-maeumjigi-18","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2026-05-13-maeumjigi-18/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-13-maeumjigi-18/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #18] Expanding Inference Tokens and Comparing 2nd Fine-Tuning Results","description":"Cover Image","date":"2026-05-13T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"fine-tuning","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/fine-tuning"},{"inline":true,"label":"inference","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/inference"},{"inline":true,"label":"max_new_tokens","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/max-new-tokens"},{"inline":true,"label":"empathy","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/empathy"},{"inline":true,"label":"capstone","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/capstone"}],"readingTime":1.7,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"jaybee","title":"Maeumjigi Developer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-05-13-maeumjigi-18","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #18] Expanding Inference Tokens and Comparing 2nd Fine-Tuning Results","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","fine-tuning","inference","max_new_tokens","empathy","capstone"],"date":"2026-05-13T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Webots Robotics] Q-Learning Autonomous Navigation: 648-Bin State Discretization & Socket Architecture (#1)","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-17-rl-qlearning-1"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #17] 2nd Fine-Tuning Complete & The Trap of the Automation Pipeline","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-12-maeumjigi-17"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nOn May 13th, after fixing the hardcoding bugs discovered yesterday, we finally resumed proper inference testing for the 2nd round fine-tuned models.\\r\\n\\r\\n### The Cause of Short Answers: Increase the Token Length!\\r\\n\\r\\nWhile meticulously reviewing the inference results, I felt that the answers from the `Llama33-70B_a8` model were still strangely short and rigid.\\r\\n\\r\\n> \\"This is one of Llama33-70B_a8\'s answers. I feel that max_seq_length and max_new_tokens are too small. I want to increase them. Looking at the VRAM status, how much can I increase them?\\"\\r\\n\\r\\nAfter calculating the available VRAM space (39.8GB at the time) with the Artificial Intelligence (AI), we boldly increased `max_new_tokens` (the maximum number of words generated) to 384. The result was a massive success!\\r\\n\\r\\n```text\\r\\n[Before] \\"It must be really hard to feel that way. It is important to talk to a professional.\\"\\r\\n[After] \\"It must be really hard to feel that way. However, having these thoughts is not a sign that you are weak, but a signal that you are carrying too much burden right now. I highly recommend slowly unraveling these emotions with a professional.\\"\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nBy simply increasing the token allowance, the model maintained its context to the end and began pouring out much deeper and warmer sentences of empathy. The model had been holding its tongue because it was trapped in a limited token count.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Alpha Value Comparison Analysis (a=8 vs a=16)\\r\\n\\r\\n![Graph Visualization](./images/02_graph.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nThe results for the biggest objective of the 2nd fine-tuning\u2014the performance difference based on the LoRA hyperparameter `alpha`\u2014were also out. By analyzing the logs piled up in the `fine_tuning_2` folder, we generated graphs like `05_alpha_comparison_daily.png`. With `rank=16` fixed, we clearly visualized how the model\'s overfitting patterns and final answer quality differed when given `alpha=8` versus `alpha=16`.\\r\\n\\r\\nUltimately, models granted the correct hyperparameters and ample token length were finally getting closer to the true \'Maeumjigi\' (Heart-keeper) we had originally planned. It seems our mid-term presentation preparations will wrap up very smoothly!"},{"id":"2026-05-12-maeumjigi-17","metadata":{"permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-12-maeumjigi-17","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2026-05-12-maeumjigi-17/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-12-maeumjigi-17/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #17] 2nd Fine-Tuning Complete & The Trap of the Automation Pipeline","description":"Cover Image","date":"2026-05-12T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"fine-tuning","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/fine-tuning"},{"inline":true,"label":"best-performance","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/best-performance"},{"inline":true,"label":"checkpoint","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/checkpoint"},{"inline":true,"label":"hardcoding","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/hardcoding"},{"inline":true,"label":"capstone","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/capstone"}],"readingTime":1.62,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"jaybee","title":"Maeumjigi Developer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-05-12-maeumjigi-17","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #17] 2nd Fine-Tuning Complete & The Trap of the Automation Pipeline","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","fine-tuning","best-performance","checkpoint","hardcoding","capstone"],"date":"2026-05-12T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #18] Expanding Inference Tokens and Comparing 2nd Fine-Tuning Results","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-13-maeumjigi-18"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #16] Fatal Mistake, 2nd Fine-Tuning, and React Native App Launch","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-08-maeumjigi-16"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nThe 2nd round of fine-tuning (`fine_tuning_2`) parallel training, which was ambitiously started to correct the LoRA hyperparameter mistake (alpha/rank setup error), finally ended on May 12th!\\r\\n\\r\\n### Extracting the Best Checkpoint and Building the Inference Pipeline\\r\\n\\r\\nThe fine-tuning for 3 models with 6 hyperparameter combinations was all completed. I immediately compared the `eval_loss` to extract the best performing checkpoints and moved them to the `adapter` folders for each model.\\r\\n\\r\\nI also created a new `run_test_20_30.sh` script to run evaluation and result comparison all at once in the background on the server.\\r\\n\\r\\n> \\"Currently, the HCX-Text-1p5B models generated 12 outputs in 15 minutes. GPU Util is 1.6GB per graphics card...\\"\\r\\n\\r\\nHowever, the inference speed was still hopeless. Even the lightest 1.5B model couldn\'t even generate 1 answer per minute. The VRAM usage was only 1.6GB, meaning a bottleneck occurred because the Batch Size was set to 1. On top of this, server storage issues forced us to urgently migrate the project\'s base path from `~/home/` to `~/home/data/temporary/`, causing a huge fuss to modify all script paths.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Falling into the Trap of Hardcoding\\r\\n\\r\\n![Hardcoding Bug](./images/02_bug.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nAfter painstakingly running all the evaluation codes, I discovered a horrifying fact.\\r\\n\\r\\n> \\"Why is MODEL_ALIAS hardcoded to \'Qwen35-9B_a16\' in 20_eval_ft_models.ipynb?\\"\\r\\n> \\"So in the server logs, even for 20_eval_HCX-Text-1p5B_a8.ipynb, MODEL_ALIAS was defined as \'Qwen35-9B_a16\'. You\'re telling me it\'s normal that the filename and model name are different?\\"\\r\\n\\r\\nYes. While writing the evaluation automation script, I accidentally hardcoded `MODEL_ALIAS`. The script diligently copied files to test the HCX model, but inside the code, it kept repeatedly loading only the `Qwen35-9B_a16` model and doing the same inference over and over!\\r\\n\\r\\nIt was a day that made me realize once again that fine-tuning is not a science, but a battle of meticulousness. I\'ll have to fix this hardcoded bug and run the inferences again tomorrow."},{"id":"2026-05-08-maeumjigi-16","metadata":{"permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-08-maeumjigi-16","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2026-05-08-maeumjigi-16/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-08-maeumjigi-16/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #16] Fatal Mistake, 2nd Fine-Tuning, and React Native App Launch","description":"Cover Image","date":"2026-05-08T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"fine-tuning","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/fine-tuning"},{"inline":true,"label":"LoRA","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/lo-ra"},{"inline":true,"label":"hyperparameters","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/hyperparameters"},{"inline":true,"label":"ReactNative","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/react-native"},{"inline":true,"label":"app-development","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/app-development"},{"inline":true,"label":"frontend","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/frontend"}],"readingTime":2.27,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"jaybee","title":"Maeumjigi Developer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-05-08-maeumjigi-16","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #16] Fatal Mistake, 2nd Fine-Tuning, and React Native App Launch","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","fine-tuning","LoRA","hyperparameters","ReactNative","app-development","frontend"],"date":"2026-05-08T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #17] 2nd Fine-Tuning Complete & The Trap of the Automation Pipeline","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-12-maeumjigi-17"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #15] Visualizing Fine-Tuning Results of 5 Models & Local Environment Setup","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-07-maeumjigi-15"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nMay 8th was the day we were building our capstone design mid-term presentation (PPT) based on the fine-tuning results. However, while happily filling in the slides, I discovered a massive, chilling mistake.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Discovering the Fatal LoRA Hyperparameter Mistake\\r\\n\\r\\nI was refining the model structure diagrams and hyperparameter info with the Artificial Intelligence (AI) for the PPT.\\r\\n\\r\\n> \\"Looking at the fine-tuning examples, the answers are shorter than the base model. I think this is because setting LoRA\'s r to 8 and alpha to 16 reduced the intermediate features too much...\\"\\r\\n> \\"Wait, it was a/r? I thought it was r/a...??? I really applied LoRA greedily...\\"\\r\\n\\r\\nWhen fine-tuning with LoRA, it\'s standard to set the `alpha` value equal to or twice the `rank` value. However, I completely misunderstood the scaling ratio equation, setting it in a way that either severely suppressed or excessively amplified the weight updates. No wonder the fine-tuned models\' answers were unnaturally short and chopped off\u2014the fault lay right at my fingertips.\\r\\n\\r\\n### The Decision for 2nd Round Fine-Tuning (fine_tuning_2)\\r\\n\\r\\nAlthough we already had the 1st round results, we couldn\'t just move on for a perfect \'Maeumjigi\'. I immediately created a `fine_tuning_2` folder and decided on a 2nd round of fine-tuning.\\r\\n\\r\\n> \\"The models to use this time are HCX-Text-1p5B, Llama33-70B, and Qwen35-9B. Train on MIG-0 with rank=16, alpha=16, and on MIG-1 with rank=16, a=8.\\"\\r\\n> \\"Extract the top 2000 longest entries from Daily_train_dataset.jsonl and create a file named Daily_train_dataset_long.jsonl.\\"\\r\\n\\r\\nTo prevent the answers from becoming too short, we extracted only the top 2000 datasets with the longest text length. We also split our MIG (Multi-Instance GPU) resources to run parallel training, directly comparing the performance differences based on the rank and alpha ratios.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Starting Frontend (App) Development While the Backend Trains\\r\\n\\r\\n![App Development](./images/02_app.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nWhile the models fell back into the cycle of training, I didn\'t just sit around. I finally started developing the frontend app where actual users will meet \'Maeumjigi\'!\\r\\n\\r\\n> \\"I need to show a QR code to run maeum-app... but when I type npx go start, I only get an error message. How and where can the QR code be shown?\\"\\r\\n\\r\\nI created the `maeum-app` project using React Native (Expo) and struggled to launch the local server and connect it to my smartphone. I even mistakenly typed `npx go start` instead of `expo`, but I finally succeeded in pulling up the Expo QR code and setting up the app icons (`assets/logo.png`). It was a thrilling day where the brain of the backend (AI) and the face of the frontend (App) were being built simultaneously."},{"id":"2026-05-07-maeumjigi-15","metadata":{"permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-07-maeumjigi-15","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2026-05-07-maeumjigi-15/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-07-maeumjigi-15/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #15] Visualizing Fine-Tuning Results of 5 Models & Local Environment Setup","description":"Cover Image","date":"2026-05-07T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"fine-tuning","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/fine-tuning"},{"inline":true,"label":"results-analysis","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/results-analysis"},{"inline":true,"label":"visualization","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/visualization"},{"inline":true,"label":"Jupyter","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/jupyter"},{"inline":true,"label":"capstone","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/capstone"},{"inline":true,"label":"Artificial Intelligence (AI)","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/artificial-intelligence-ai"}],"readingTime":1.66,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"jaybee","title":"Maeumjigi Developer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-05-07-maeumjigi-15","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #15] Visualizing Fine-Tuning Results of 5 Models & Local Environment Setup","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","fine-tuning","results-analysis","visualization","Jupyter","capstone","Artificial Intelligence (AI)"],"date":"2026-05-07T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #16] Fatal Mistake, 2nd Fine-Tuning, and React Native App Launch","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-08-maeumjigi-16"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #14] All Fine-Tuning Completed and Parallel Inference (Eval) Pipeline Built","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-06-maeumjigi-14"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nMay 7th was the day we synthesized the fine-tuning results of our 5 models (Llama3.3-70B, DSR1-70B, HCX-Omni-8B, Qwen35-9B, HCX-Text-1.5B) and proceeded with full-scale visualization and local environment setup.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Visualization and Local Setup for Model Comparison\\r\\n\\r\\nTo comfortably review the result comparison code (which was previously running on the heavy server) on my personal laptop, I created a new notebook, `31_comparison_local.ipynb`.\\r\\n\\r\\n> \\"Can\'t 30_comparison.ipynb run on my laptop too?\\"\\r\\n> \\"Please tell me how to install pandas, matplotlib, seaborn, and bert_score on the currently selected kernel.\\"\\r\\n\\r\\nWhile setting up the local Jupyter kernel in VSCode and installing the necessary libraries, I faced frequent Kernel Crashes and annoying errors like `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named \'ft_utils\'`. However, I resolved them step-by-step by fixing folder paths. I also successfully recovered the missing `ft_metrics.json` files for some models (like DSR1-70B) using a script.\\r\\n\\r\\n### 5-Model Comprehensive Report and Graph Visualization\\r\\n\\r\\nThe most important task was visualizing the performance of these 5 models so they could be compared at a glance. Together with the AI, we established a plan named `5MODELS_ORGANIZATION_PLAN_present.md` and began systematically organizing the data.\\r\\n\\r\\n> \\"What is the difference between the faint line and the thick line? Bring the training loss and eval loss for each model and visualize them as graphs.\\"\\r\\n> \\"Remove the thick line and make the faint line clearer and save it.\\"\\r\\n\\r\\nI removed the unnecessary trend lines (thick lines) from the matplotlib graphs generated by the AI, finally extracting `03_training_loss_all.png` and `04_eval_loss_all.png` which neatly gathered the Training and Eval Loss curves of all models in one place. We also perfectly documented graphs analyzing the LoRA Rank Decomposition for each model, as well as a GPU memory usage chart (`02_gpu_memory.png`).\\r\\n\\r\\n![Graph Visualization](./images/02_graph.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nNow, based on these graphs, we can beautifully decorate our capstone design mid-term presentation materials for next week. Which of these 5 models will ultimately be selected as the best quality \'Maeumjigi\' chatbot?"},{"id":"2026-05-06-maeumjigi-14","metadata":{"permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-06-maeumjigi-14","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2026-05-06-maeumjigi-14/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-06-maeumjigi-14/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #14] All Fine-Tuning Completed and Parallel Inference (Eval) Pipeline Built","description":"Cover Image","date":"2026-05-06T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"fine-tuning","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/fine-tuning"},{"inline":true,"label":"HCX-Omni-8B","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/hcx-omni-8-b"},{"inline":true,"label":"Qwen35-9B","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/qwen-35-9-b"},{"inline":true,"label":"model-evaluation","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/model-evaluation"},{"inline":true,"label":"inference","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/inference"},{"inline":true,"label":"parallel-processing","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/parallel-processing"}],"readingTime":1.61,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"jaybee","title":"Maeumjigi Developer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-05-06-maeumjigi-14","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #14] All Fine-Tuning Completed and Parallel Inference (Eval) Pipeline Built","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","fine-tuning","HCX-Omni-8B","Qwen35-9B","model-evaluation","inference","parallel-processing"],"date":"2026-05-06T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #15] Visualizing Fine-Tuning Results of 5 Models & Local Environment Setup","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-07-maeumjigi-15"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #13] Limits of 70B Training and the Pivot to Mid-Size Models (HCX, Qwen)","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-04-maeumjigi-13"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nOn May 6th, we finally escaped the long swamp of fine-tuning! After several nights of work and hyperparameter tuning, the training for mid-size models like **HCX-Omni-8B** and **Qwen35-9B** was successfully completed.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Fine-Tuning Complete, Analyzing the Logs\\r\\n\\r\\nAs the training progressed, we could see the loss values decreasing stably.\\r\\n\\r\\n```text\\r\\nstep= 1200  loss=0.9698  lr=1.88e-04  gpu=7.7GB\\r\\nstep= 1250  loss=0.8523  lr=1.86e-04  gpu=7.7GB\\r\\n...\\r\\nstep= 1650  loss=0.8783  lr=1.73e-04  gpu=7.7GB\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nHowever, we had a brief moment of confusion trying to figure out if this was `train loss` or `validation loss`, asking the Artificial Intelligence (AI), \\"Does our code not record eval loss?\\" We realized the evaluation loss logging might have been missed, but since the training was already fully complete (\\"All fine-tuning is complete, proceeding with evaluation\\"), we immediately moved on to the inference and evaluation (Eval) phase using the latest generated checkpoint models.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Building a Parallel Inference Environment\\r\\n\\r\\nWe started generation testing using the `20_eval_ft_models.ipynb` notebook to see how each model responded to user dialogues. We unified the `max_new_tokens` for inference to a generous 1024, constantly worrying if another OOM would occur.\\r\\n\\r\\nBut the inference speed was much slower than expected.\\r\\n> \\"We used two graphics cards for training, so why is inference only using one?\\"\\r\\n\\r\\nTo utilize 100% of the server\'s GPU resources, with the AI\'s help, we wrote a new `run_eval_parallel.sh` script to process inferences in parallel. This drastically reduced the model inference time, and we successfully built a pipeline that automatically aggregates the inference results and perplexity of each model into an Excel file.\\r\\n\\r\\n![Evaluation Pipeline](./images/02_eval.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nAlthough an error occurred because VRAM was not properly released when transitioning from DSR1-70B inference to the next model, we resolved it by isolating the environments with the parallel script. Now, it\'s finally time to compare the actual answer quality of each model!"},{"id":"2026-05-04-maeumjigi-13","metadata":{"permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-04-maeumjigi-13","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2026-05-04-maeumjigi-13/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-04-maeumjigi-13/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #13] Limits of 70B Training and the Pivot to Mid-Size Models (HCX, Qwen)","description":"Cover Image","date":"2026-05-04T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"fine-tuning","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/fine-tuning"},{"inline":true,"label":"HCX-Omni-8B","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/hcx-omni-8-b"},{"inline":true,"label":"Qwen35-9B","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/qwen-35-9-b"},{"inline":true,"label":"checkpoints","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/checkpoints"},{"inline":true,"label":"hyperparameters","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/hyperparameters"},{"inline":true,"label":"Artificial Intelligence (AI)","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/artificial-intelligence-ai"}],"readingTime":1.81,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"jaybee","title":"Maeumjigi Developer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-05-04-maeumjigi-13","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #13] Limits of 70B Training and the Pivot to Mid-Size Models (HCX, Qwen)","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","fine-tuning","HCX-Omni-8B","Qwen35-9B","checkpoints","hyperparameters","Artificial Intelligence (AI)"],"date":"2026-05-04T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #14] All Fine-Tuning Completed and Parallel Inference (Eval) Pipeline Built","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-06-maeumjigi-14"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #12] 70B Massive Model Fine-Tuning and GPU Memory (OOM) Struggle","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-03-maeumjigi-12"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nAfter experiencing the OOM (Out of Memory) hell during the fine-tuning of massive 70B models, on May 4th, we attempted a bold change in strategy. By drastically reducing the batch size, we barely managed to push the 70B models (Llama3.3, DeepSeek-R1) to `checkpoint-1200` and `1300`, but the training time was absurdly long, resulting in terrible efficiency.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Pivoting to Mid-Size Models (1.5B ~ 9B)\\r\\n\\r\\nConsidering our physical server resources and the capstone project deadline, we decided it was too risky to rely solely on the 70B models. Therefore, we shifted our focus to relatively lighter mid-size models like **HCX-Omni-8B, Qwen35-9B, and HCX-Text-1.5B**.\\r\\n\\r\\n> \\"It\'s taking a long time to train HCX-Omni-8B, Qwen35-9B, and HCX-Text-1p5B with the current hyperparameters. Are these parameters generally used for these models?\\"\\r\\n> \\"How does increasing the batch size affect the training?\\"\\r\\n\\r\\nThings weren\'t perfectly smooth just because the models were smaller. To speed up training, we increased the batch size and endlessly debated with the AI to find the optimal hyperparameters for each model size. We first evaluated the training results of the smallest model, HCX-Text-1.5B, to set our tuning direction.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Checkpoint Saving Errors and Kernel Version Issues\\r\\n\\r\\nWhile smoothly training HCX-Omni-8B, we hit another snag while trying to save a checkpoint at step 50.\\r\\n\\r\\n```python\\r\\n[RANK 0] Detected kernel version 5.4.0, which is below the recommended minimum of 5.5.0; this can cause the process to hang.\\r\\n...\\r\\n![\uC0AC\uC9C4 02](./images/02_error.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nJSONDecodeError Traceback (most recent call last)\\r\\n---\x3e 60 trainer.train(resume_from_checkpoint=...)\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nAlong with a warning that the OS kernel version was too low and could cause the process to hang, we faced a horrific `JSONDecodeError` because the saved checkpoint file was corrupted, preventing us from resuming training.\\r\\n\\r\\n> \\"If the models stop during training, do they resume from where they stopped?\\"\\r\\n\\r\\nFortunately, the Hugging Face `Trainer` provides a feature to resume training from checkpoints (`resume_from_checkpoint`), but it\'s useless if the checkpoint file itself is corrupted. Despite reducing the model size, we spent the entire day wrestling with numerous framework and infrastructure errors. Will we ever reach a stable training orbit?"},{"id":"2026-05-03-maeumjigi-12","metadata":{"permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-03-maeumjigi-12","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2026-05-03-maeumjigi-12/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-03-maeumjigi-12/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #12] 70B Massive Model Fine-Tuning and GPU Memory (OOM) Struggle","description":"Cover Image","date":"2026-05-03T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"fine-tuning","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/fine-tuning"},{"inline":true,"label":"Llama3.3-70B","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/llama-3-3-70-b"},{"inline":true,"label":"DeepSeek-R1-70B","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/deep-seek-r-1-70-b"},{"inline":true,"label":"OOM","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/oom"},{"inline":true,"label":"troubleshooting","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/troubleshooting"},{"inline":true,"label":"GPU","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/gpu"}],"readingTime":1.69,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"jaybee","title":"Maeumjigi Developer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-05-03-maeumjigi-12","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #12] 70B Massive Model Fine-Tuning and GPU Memory (OOM) Struggle","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","fine-tuning","Llama3.3-70B","DeepSeek-R1-70B","OOM","troubleshooting","GPU"],"date":"2026-05-03T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #13] Limits of 70B Training and the Pivot to Mid-Size Models (HCX, Qwen)","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-04-maeumjigi-13"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #11] Evaluating M6 Model Trained on Synthetic Data (v5)","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-04-19-maeumjigi-11"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nOn May 3, having gained enough experience with the 8B models, our team finally challenged the ultimate kings of performance: fine-tuning massive **70B (70 billion parameters)** models. We attempted to train both Llama 3.3 70B and DeepSeek-R1 70B, but as expected, we hit a massive hardware resource wall.\\r\\n\\r\\n### 70B Models and the Terror of OOM (Out of Memory)\\r\\n\\r\\n- **Problem**: As soon as we ambitiously uploaded and ran the 70B fine-tuning code on the server, errors started popping up.\\r\\n- **Process**: \\r\\n  > \\"I copied the server\'s fine_tuning folder and sub-files. Please check the copied files and identify the cause of the error.\\"\\r\\n  > \\"Do we not need to modify DeepSeek\'s 70B model? Please check for potential errors.\\"\\r\\n- **Result**: Our brief moment of relaxation, analyzing `train_log.jsonl` and asking for estimated completion times, was shattered when the 70B models instantly devoured all the GPU memory. (OOM error occurred!)\\r\\n\\r\\n### The GPU Memory Diet Operation\\r\\n\\r\\n- **Problem**: \\"The GPU memory usage is already at MAX.\\" We had to somehow reduce the memory usage so the training wouldn\'t crash.\\r\\n- **Process**:\\r\\n  > \\"Modified to save_steps = 50, save_total_limit = 10, eval_steps = 50, patience = 10.\\"\\r\\n  > \\"Please evaluate the configured early stopping conditions.\\"\\r\\n  > \\"I set the max_seq_length of both 70B models to 512 in ft_configs.yaml, but the server\'s nvitop still shows memory usage at max. Why is that?\\"\\r\\n- **Result**: We drastically reduced the context length processed at once (`max_seq_length`) to 512 and heavily modified hyperparameters to reduce unnecessary saving. However, monitoring with `nvitop` showed that VRAM was still gasping at its absolute limit. Eventually, the horrific phenomenon of the training freezing around steps 100 and 150 repeated itself.\\r\\n\\r\\n![Terminal Screenshot](./images/02_term.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nTaming a massive beast like 70B is truly not easy. We are constantly throwing error logs at the Artificial Intelligence (AI) to find solutions, but it seems we need endless testing to see if we can overcome the physical VRAM limits with software optimization techniques (LoRA, Quantization, etc.). Will we succeed in fine-tuning the 70B models? To be continued in the next log!"},{"id":"2026-04-19-maeumjigi-11","metadata":{"permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-04-19-maeumjigi-11","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2026-04-19-maeumjigi-11/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-04-19-maeumjigi-11/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #11] Evaluating M6 Model Trained on Synthetic Data (v5)","description":"Cover Image","date":"2026-04-19T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"synthetic-data","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/synthetic-data"},{"inline":true,"label":"model-evaluation","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/model-evaluation"},{"inline":true,"label":"M6","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/m-6"},{"inline":true,"label":"FAISS","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/faiss"},{"inline":true,"label":"weekly-report","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/weekly-report"}],"readingTime":2.04,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"jaybee","title":"Maeumjigi Developer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-04-19-maeumjigi-11","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #11] Evaluating M6 Model Trained on Synthetic Data (v5)","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","synthetic-data","model-evaluation","M6","FAISS","weekly-report"],"date":"2026-04-19T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #12] 70B Massive Model Fine-Tuning and GPU Memory (OOM) Struggle","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-05-03-maeumjigi-12"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #10] Lifting Application Programming Interface (API) Limits and Resuming Data Synthesis","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-04-14-maeumjigi-10"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nApril 19 is the day we evaluated the performance of the model (M6) trained on the virtual synthetic dataset (v5) we painstakingly created, and wrote the 7th-week weekly report based on it. Was the data we generated while enduring a $10 bill bomb worth it?\\r\\n\\r\\n### A Real Showdown of Model Performance: M0 vs M5 vs M6\\r\\n\\r\\n- **Problem**: To persuade the advising professor and team members, we needed a weekly report that included quantitative test results and intuitive comparison examples, rather than simply claiming \\"it got better.\\"\\r\\n- **Process**: \\r\\n  > \\"Please rewrite the report. The main point is the comparison between M5 based on v4 and M6 based on v5... Include examples of each dataset.\\"\\r\\n  > \\"When describing the test results, include explanations with examples of the differences between the un-tuned base model (M0) and M6.\\"\\r\\n- **Result**: The Artificial Intelligence (AI) analyzed the text results generated by the M0, M5, and M6 models and extracted a comparison file (`m0_m6_compare.json`). The results showed that the base Llama 3.1 model (M0) was somewhat awkward in Korean counseling, and while M5 (trained on refined data v4) improved, it lacked depth. In contrast, the M6 model trained on high-quality synthetic data (v5) perfectly commanded the warm and professional tone typical of a psychological counselor, deeply empathizing with the user\'s emotions.\\r\\n\\r\\n### The 7th-Week Weekly Report Written by AI (DOCX)\\r\\n\\r\\n- **Process**:\\r\\n  > \\"Please write the 7th-week report focusing on the results and significance in two versions (a concise version under 5 pages, and a detailed version). Then generate docx files for each based on the two md files.\\"\\r\\n- **Result**: With a single instruction, two versions (`7th-week_report_v1_concise.md`, `7th-week_report_v2_detailed.md`) were completed in a flash, and even neatly converted into Word files.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Introducing RAG Architecture: FAISS Database (DB) Construction Evaluation\\r\\n\\r\\n- **Process**:\\r\\n  > \\"I performed FAISS_DB construction and wrote FAISS_DB_construction_report.docx. Please evaluate our plan and the results of the report.\\"\\r\\n- **Result**: In addition to fine-tuning, which tweaks the model\'s weights, we introduced a FAISS vector database for RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) to reduce hallucinations by referencing external knowledge. I asked the AI to evaluate the structure I built, and received positive feedback (`FAISS_DB_evaluation.md`).\\r\\n\\r\\n![Terminal Screenshot](./images/02_term.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nToday was a day when all the hard work paid off. The thrill of confirming that the trained AI model actually became noticeably smarter is incredible! With model performance proven and the FAISS DB successfully integrated, the Maeumjigi chatbot is one step closer to completion."},{"id":"2026-04-14-maeumjigi-10","metadata":{"permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-04-14-maeumjigi-10","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2026-04-14-maeumjigi-10/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-04-14-maeumjigi-10/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #10] Lifting Application Programming Interface (API) Limits and Resuming Data Synthesis","description":"Cover Image","date":"2026-04-14T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"API-limits","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/api-limits"},{"inline":true,"label":"synthetic-data","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/synthetic-data"},{"inline":true,"label":"data-generation","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/data-generation"},{"inline":true,"label":"troubleshooting","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/troubleshooting"}],"readingTime":1.48,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"jaybee","title":"Maeumjigi Developer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-04-14-maeumjigi-10","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #10] Lifting Application Programming Interface (API) Limits and Resuming Data Synthesis","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","API-limits","synthetic-data","data-generation","troubleshooting"],"date":"2026-04-14T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #11] Evaluating M6 Model Trained on Synthetic Data (v5)","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-04-19-maeumjigi-11"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #9] Llama 3.1 Fine-Tuning Error Struggle and Week 6 Mid-term Presentation","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-04-09-maeumjigi-9"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nApril 14 is the day we resumed the synthetic data (v5) generation work, which had been temporarily halted since the bill bomb incident. We increased the API billing limit (lifted the restriction) and ran the system, but once again, it didn\'t go smoothly.\\r\\n\\r\\n### \\"API limit lifted, please resume operation!\\"\\r\\n\\r\\n- **Problem**: During our last attempt at massive data generation, the script was forcibly stopped because we exceeded the monthly API limit.\\r\\n- **Process**: \\r\\n  > \\"Proceed with work according to v5_emotion_data_generation_commands.md\\"\\r\\n  > \\"API limit lifted, please resume operation\\" (23:32)\\r\\n  > \\"API limit lifted, please resume operation\\" (00:11)\\r\\n- **Result**: We logged into the Claude API console, boldly increased the billing limit, and lifted the restriction. We repeatedly instructed the Artificial Intelligence (AI) to resume generating data from where it stopped and restarted the pipeline.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Repairing the Broken Pipeline and Unmanned Automation\\r\\n\\r\\n- **Problem**: The API limit was lifted, but for some reason, new data was not being generated properly.\\r\\n- **Process**:\\r\\n  > \\"Nothing is being generated. Please check\\"\\r\\n  > \\"When the task is complete, automatically disconnect the API after checking the results (All related permissions are granted, so no need to ask for permission)\\"\\r\\n- **Result**: We made the AI independently check and fix the tangles that occurred in the middle of the generation logic (tracking `v5_sync_progress.json`). Since we couldn\'t stand by and fix errors every time, we gave an unmanned automation instruction: \\"I will give you all permissions, so when the task is done, automatically disconnect the API on your own and safely shut down.\\"\\r\\n\\r\\n![Terminal Screenshot](./images/02_term.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nToday was a tough journey of balancing money (API fees), time, and automation scripts for data preprocessing. If the data generation is completed safely, we can finally move on to the next step, right?\\r\\nSee you in the next dev log!"},{"id":"2026-04-09-maeumjigi-9","metadata":{"permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-04-09-maeumjigi-9","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2026-04-09-maeumjigi-9/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-04-09-maeumjigi-9/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #9] Llama 3.1 Fine-Tuning Error Struggle and Week 6 Mid-term Presentation","description":"Cover Image","date":"2026-04-09T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"Llama3.1","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/llama-3-1"},{"inline":true,"label":"fine-tuning","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/fine-tuning"},{"inline":true,"label":"troubleshooting","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/troubleshooting"},{"inline":true,"label":"PPT-generation","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/ppt-generation"},{"inline":true,"label":"ERD","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/erd"}],"readingTime":2.06,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"jaybee","title":"Maeumjigi Developer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-04-09-maeumjigi-9","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #9] Llama 3.1 Fine-Tuning Error Struggle and Week 6 Mid-term Presentation","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","Llama3.1","fine-tuning","troubleshooting","PPT-generation","ERD"],"date":"2026-04-09T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #10] Lifting Application Programming Interface (API) Limits and Resuming Data Synthesis","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-04-14-maeumjigi-10"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #8] Application Programming Interface (API) Bill Bomb Incident and Preparing Colab Fine-Tuning","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-04-08-maeumjigi-8"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nOn April 9, using the Colab notebook we ambitiously completed yesterday, we finally attempted the first training (fine-tuning) of our artificial intelligence model (Llama 3.1). However, reality was not that easy, and we had to face numerous errors. It was also a tough day as we had to simultaneously create the presentation materials for the 6th week of the Capstone Design project.\\r\\n\\r\\n### The Endless Struggle with Colab Fine-Tuning Errors\\r\\n\\r\\n- **Problem**: As soon as we executed `llama31_finetuning_colab.ipynb` created yesterday, various errors poured out.\\r\\n- **Process**: \\r\\n  > \\"Why is an error occurring during M1 fine-tuning in llama31_finetuning_colab (3).ipynb?\\"\\r\\n  > \\"The executed result contains the eos_token. Why is this problem occurring?\\"\\r\\n- **Result**: A series of problems occurred, such as the special token (`eos_token`) not being processed correctly when the Artificial Intelligence (AI) model spit out the training data, and memory overflow issues. We repeated the process of copying and pasting the entire error log to Claude to get solutions and modifying the code multiple times. Finally, after many twists and turns, the training was completed, and we were able to extract a meaningful result graph called `\uD559\uC2B5\uB370\uC774\uD130\uBCC4\uD559\uC2B5\uACB0\uACFC.png`!\\r\\n\\r\\n### The AI Assistant\'s Presentation (PPT) Creation\\r\\n\\r\\n- **Problem**: While staying up all night training the model, we had to immediately create a PPT on the 6th-week frontend and database progress for the presentation next week.\\r\\n- **Process**:\\r\\n  > \\"Capstone_Week6_Frontend.pptx is a collection of images introducing the overview so far. Please use this to create a presentation for this week\'s progress.\\"\\r\\n  > \\"Please express the tables of the database design so that the relationships can be seen, referring to the tables implemented in the backend... The table is too large and messy. Please place the User table in the center.\\"\\r\\n- **Result**: Surprisingly, the AI wove the frontend image screenshots together to create a presentation script and slide skeleton. Even when I persistently gave feedback (centering the User table) asking it to draw a complex database ERD (Entity Relationship Diagram) based on the backend code, it perfectly organized and explained the database relationship diagram.\\r\\n\\r\\n![Terminal Screenshot](./images/02_term.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nToday was a day to feel the thrill of directly tuning the latest language model called Llama 3.1 with my own hands (and the AI\'s brain). At the same time, we are learning how to fully utilize AI not only for coding but also for creating presentation materials and designing Database (DB) architecture diagrams. How smart has our Maeumjigi AI become? We will reveal the test results next time!"},{"id":"2026-04-08-maeumjigi-8","metadata":{"permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-04-08-maeumjigi-8","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2026-04-08-maeumjigi-8/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-04-08-maeumjigi-8/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #8] Application Programming Interface (API) Bill Bomb Incident and Preparing Colab Fine-Tuning","description":"Cover Image","date":"2026-04-08T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"API-billing","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/api-billing"},{"inline":true,"label":"Llama3.1","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/llama-3-1"},{"inline":true,"label":"fine-tuning","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/fine-tuning"},{"inline":true,"label":"GoogleColab","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/google-colab"},{"inline":true,"label":"data-preprocessing","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/data-preprocessing"}],"readingTime":1.77,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"jaybee","title":"Maeumjigi Developer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-04-08-maeumjigi-8","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #8] Application Programming Interface (API) Bill Bomb Incident and Preparing Colab Fine-Tuning","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","API-billing","Llama3.1","fine-tuning","GoogleColab","data-preprocessing"],"date":"2026-04-08T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #9] Llama 3.1 Fine-Tuning Error Struggle and Week 6 Mid-term Presentation","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-04-09-maeumjigi-9"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #7] Synthetic Data (v5) Generation Optimization and Frontend Integration","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-04-07-maeumjigi-7"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nApril 8th was the day we experienced the biggest crisis and a \'laugh-and-cry\' happening while working on the Maeumjigi project. It was the **Claude API monthly limit exceeded & bill bomb incident**.\\r\\n\\r\\n### The Betrayal of the Background: \\"$1 for 10 records?\\"\\r\\n\\r\\n- **Problem**: Relieved that we had finished optimization yesterday, we left the massive test dataset generation script running in the background unattended.\\r\\n- **Process**: \\r\\n  > \\"Problem: Billed exceeding the monthly limit. I left it running thinking it was just working in the background, and this happened.\\"\\r\\n  > \\"No way, it costs $1 to generate 10 records? Does that make sense?\\"\\r\\n- **Result**: During the process of synthesizing high-quality counseling data using an Large Language Model (LLM), the prompt\'s context (input tokens) accumulated, causing the cost to increase exponentially. The script halted because the monthly billing limit was exceeded. We urgently instructed the Artificial Intelligence (AI), \\"Immediately add a feature to print the estimated consumption, cost, and progress in real-time to the console, and prevent limit exceedance.\\" We learned the hard way how crucial billing monitoring is when using clouds or APIs.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Writing a Colab Notebook for Llama 3.1 Training\\r\\n\\r\\n- **Problem**: As the data was getting ready, we now needed the code to actually fine-tune the Llama 3.1 model. Since our local PC\'s GPU wasn\'t nearly enough, we had to use the Google Colab environment.\\r\\n- **Process**:\\r\\n  > \\"Please write a python notebook to execute llama31_finetuning_experiment_plan_revised.md in Colab.\\"\\r\\n  > \\"Split the training data only into train/val instead of train/val/test, using a 9:1 ratio.\\"\\r\\n- **Result**: The AI independently read the training plan and wrote a perfect Jupyter Notebook code called `llama31_finetuning_colab.ipynb`. It perfectly implemented logic including setting up the latest lightweight libraries like Unsloth, and splitting the dataset into a 9:1 ratio for training and validation.\\r\\n\\r\\n![Terminal Screenshot](./images/02_term.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nToday was a day of overcoming a realistic barrier of the project, paying a terrifying initiation fee called the API bill bomb. However, in return, we completed an excellent dataset and a Colab notebook for fine-tuning. Finally, the next step is to train the real artificial intelligence model!"},{"id":"2026-04-07-maeumjigi-7","metadata":{"permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-04-07-maeumjigi-7","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2026-04-07-maeumjigi-7/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-04-07-maeumjigi-7/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #7] Synthetic Data (v5) Generation Optimization and Frontend Integration","description":"Cover Image","date":"2026-04-07T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"synthetic-data","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/synthetic-data"},{"inline":true,"label":"ClaudeAPI","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/claude-api"},{"inline":true,"label":"data-generation","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/data-generation"},{"inline":true,"label":"frontend","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/frontend"},{"inline":true,"label":"cost-optimization","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/cost-optimization"}],"readingTime":1.94,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"jaybee","title":"Maeumjigi Developer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-04-07-maeumjigi-7","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #7] Synthetic Data (v5) Generation Optimization and Frontend Integration","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","synthetic-data","ClaudeAPI","data-generation","frontend","cost-optimization"],"date":"2026-04-07T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #8] Application Programming Interface (API) Bill Bomb Incident and Preparing Colab Fine-Tuning","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-04-08-maeumjigi-8"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #6] Preprocessing Final Report Automation and Frontend Kickoff","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-04-05-maeumjigi-6"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nApril 7 was the day we attempted integration by running the frontend code built by a teammate in a local environment, \\r\\nand also the day we launched a massive script using Artificial Intelligence (AI) to \'synthesize\' 60,000 new counseling data points to fill our dataset gaps.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Filling in the Blanks: Generating AI Synthetic Data (v5)\\r\\n\\r\\n- **Problem**: The previously refined data (v4) alone lacked specific emotions or detailed counseling scenarios. To solve this, we wanted to use the Claude Application Programming Interface (API) to generate 60,000 virtual, high-quality counseling conversations that fit the Llama 3.1 training format (System, User, Assistant).\\r\\n- **Process**: \\r\\n  > \\"We want to create a v5 dataset entirely generated by Claude. Please make a plan.\\"\\r\\n  > \\"I want to obtain 60,000 dataset records generated by Claude in jsonl format.\\"\\r\\n- **Result**: We established `v5_plan.md` and started generating data. However, the API call cost quickly became a stumbling block again.\\r\\n\\r\\n### The Never-ending War with Costs: Prompt Optimization\\r\\n\\r\\n- **Problem**: While trying to make the AI author 60,000 data points, the cost issue we experienced a few days ago resurfaced. During the script execution, $10 in credits evaporated in an instant.\\r\\n- **Process**:\\r\\n  > \\"Will token usage decrease if we proceed exactly like step8_generate_v5_optimized.py?\\"\\r\\n  > \\"We have already consumed $10 of our credit and are only at this progress level.\\"\\r\\n- **Result**: We instructed the AI to perform prompt engineering and code optimization (`step8_generate_v5_optimized.py`) to reduce costs, preventing the waste of output tokens and adding logic to safely save generation progress.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Handover and Execution of Teammate\'s Frontend Code\\r\\n\\r\\n- **Process**: \\r\\n  > \\"This project was created by a teammate; what should I do to run it?\\"\\r\\n  > \\"Error occurred during Database (DB) migration...\\"\\r\\n  > \\"Execution up to frontend step 6 complete. Please tell me how to shut it down and the simple procedure to run it again.\\"\\r\\n- **Result**: We took over the `mental-health` repository written by a teammate, ran it locally, and resolved DB migration errors. Now, the backend AI model and the frontend are ready to meet.\\r\\n\\r\\n![Terminal Screenshot](./images/02_term.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nToday was a meaningful day where, moving beyond simple data preprocessing, we directly implemented the latest deep learning trend of **Synthetic Data Generation**, making an AI write the textbooks (data) to teach another AI.\\r\\nIt was a day of achieving optimization amidst the horror of pouring bills and safely launching the frontend server!"},{"id":"2026-04-05-maeumjigi-6","metadata":{"permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-04-05-maeumjigi-6","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2026-04-05-maeumjigi-6/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-04-05-maeumjigi-6/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #6] Preprocessing Final Report Automation and Frontend Kickoff","description":"Cover Image","date":"2026-04-05T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"final-report","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/final-report"},{"inline":true,"label":"automation","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/automation"},{"inline":true,"label":"Next.js","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/next-js"},{"inline":true,"label":"capstone","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/capstone"},{"inline":true,"label":"data-preprocessing","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/data-preprocessing"}],"readingTime":1.56,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"jaybee","title":"Maeumjigi Developer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-04-05-maeumjigi-6","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #6] Preprocessing Final Report Automation and Frontend Kickoff","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","final-report","automation","Next.js","capstone","data-preprocessing"],"date":"2026-04-05T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #7] Synthetic Data (v5) Generation Optimization and Frontend Integration","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-04-07-maeumjigi-7"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #5] Purifying 100,000 Emotion Data Points and the Pressure of Application Programming Interface (API) Costs","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-04-03-maeumjigi-5"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nApril 5th marks the day we wrapped up the arduous data preprocessing journey (v1~v4) by generating a final report, \\r\\nwhile simultaneously kicking off the frontend project that will become the face of the Maeumjigi web service.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Perfect Documentation: From Markdown to DOCX and Graphs\\r\\n\\r\\n- **Problem**: We needed to share the massive preprocessing journey with our advising professor and team members in a visually understandable way.\\r\\n- **Process**: \\r\\n  > To Claude Code: \\"Please write a final report summarizing the data preprocessing process (v1, v2, v3, v4)... Add representative examples for each version.\\"\\r\\n  > \\"Please convert this md file to a docx file... Express things that can be graphed as graphs and add them.\\"\\r\\n- **Result**: Surprisingly, the Artificial Intelligence (AI) analyzed the Python scripts and JSON data we had worked on and effortlessly wrote a core summary report in Markdown. Going even further, it independently installed Python libraries (`python-docx`, `matplotlib`), wrote a script, converted the Markdown into a neat Word (DOCX) file, and even drew and inserted emotion distribution graphs! It was a moment that drastically reduced the time wasted on documentation.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Next.js Based Frontend Project Kickoff\\r\\n\\r\\n- **Problem**: Separate from training the AI model, we needed a web interface where users could actually chat with the bot.\\r\\n- **Process**: (Background Task) We instructed the setup of a Next.js-based frontend project to provide a fast and familiar UI for users (soldiers).\\r\\n- **Result**: `package.json`, `tsconfig.json`, etc., were generated in the `maeumjigi/frontend/` path, successfully setting up the latest trending Next.js-based frontend environment.\\r\\n\\r\\n![Terminal Screenshot](./images/02_term.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nToday was the day we proved that AI\'s capabilities go far beyond simple coding, perfectly performing the role of an excellent office assistant through \'document summarization, graph visualization, and DOCX generation\'.\\r\\nHaving climbed the huge mountain of data preprocessing, we have now officially raised the anchor for the frontend development that will meet the users!"},{"id":"2026-04-03-maeumjigi-5","metadata":{"permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-04-03-maeumjigi-5","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2026-04-03-maeumjigi-5/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-04-03-maeumjigi-5/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #5] Purifying 100,000 Emotion Data Points and the Pressure of Application Programming Interface (API) Costs","description":"Cover Image","date":"2026-04-03T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"data-cleaning","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/data-cleaning"},{"inline":true,"label":"ClaudeAPI","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/claude-api"},{"inline":true,"label":"emotion-classification","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/emotion-classification"},{"inline":true,"label":"Llama3.1","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/llama-3-1"},{"inline":true,"label":"api-cost","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/api-cost"}],"readingTime":1.9,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"jaybee","title":"Maeumjigi Developer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-04-03-maeumjigi-5","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #5] Purifying 100,000 Emotion Data Points and the Pressure of Application Programming Interface (API) Costs","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","data-cleaning","ClaudeAPI","emotion-classification","Llama3.1","api-cost"],"date":"2026-04-03T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #6] Preprocessing Final Report Automation and Frontend Kickoff","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-04-05-maeumjigi-6"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #4] Full-Scale Fine-Tuning Data Preprocessing","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-04-01-maeumjigi-4"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nThis is the record of April 3, the day we began in earnest the task of refining the training conversation data to feed into the deep learning model (Llama 3.1).\\r\\nIt was also the day we ran a massive pipeline that makes the Artificial Intelligence (AI) read the text and judge the emotion on its own, going beyond simple data merging.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Reclassifying Data Emotions (v3 -> v4)\\r\\n\\r\\n- **Problem**: The emotion labeling of the existing dataset (`Emotion_train_dataset_v3`) did not perfectly align with our specific psychological counseling goals. Out of a total of 1 million data points, we needed to filter only the meaningful ones and accurately map their emotions.\\r\\n- **Process**: \\r\\n  > \\"The scope is the entire 1 million... Because Claude, as a psychological counseling expert, will read the conversation and directly map the emotion. We are filtering only what falls under our emotion classification and excluding the rest.\\"\\r\\n- **Result**: We established `v4_plan.md` and started the work. Initially targeting 100,000 lines of data, we wrote and ran a script that integrates the Claude API to grasp the conversational context and reclassify emotions.\\r\\n\\r\\n### The Horror of Large Language Model (LLM) API Billing\\r\\n\\r\\n- **Problem**: As we threw massive amounts of text data at an LLM (Claude) for evaluation, the API usage (token count) increased exponentially.\\r\\n- **Process**:\\r\\n  > \\"I\'m already using Claude with a Pro subscription, do I need a separate API?\\"\\r\\n  > \\"Is there a way to use Claude Code instead of the Anthropic API? The billing is burdensome.\\"\\r\\n  > \\"The actual cost came out to $10.27.\\"\\r\\n- **Result**: We painfully realized that, completely separate from the Claude Pro subscription ($20/month), the Anthropic API called by the Python script for automated batch processing is billed on a pay-as-you-go basis depending on usage. Just processing 100,000 test lines instantly cost us about $10.27. Realizing that processing all 1 million records would cost a fortune, it was a day we learned firsthand the importance of model optimization and data sampling.\\r\\n\\r\\n![Terminal Screenshot](./images/02_term.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nToday was the day we hit the realistic wall of \'computing costs\' inevitably encountered during deep learning data preprocessing.\\r\\nAlthough we successfully obtained high-quality, refined data (`v4_report.md`), it was a valuable experience that left us deeply contemplating how to balance cost and efficiency as we proceed with fine-tuning!"},{"id":"2026-04-01-maeumjigi-4","metadata":{"permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-04-01-maeumjigi-4","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2026-04-01-maeumjigi-4/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-04-01-maeumjigi-4/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #4] Full-Scale Fine-Tuning Data Preprocessing","description":"Cover Image","date":"2026-04-01T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"Artificial Intelligence (AI)-coding","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/artificial-intelligence-ai-coding"},{"inline":true,"label":"ClaudeCode","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/claude-code"},{"inline":true,"label":"capstone","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/capstone"},{"inline":true,"label":"fine-tuning","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/fine-tuning"},{"inline":true,"label":"data-preprocessing","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/data-preprocessing"},{"inline":true,"label":"Llama3.1","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/llama-3-1"}],"readingTime":1.62,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"jaybee","title":"Maeumjigi Developer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-04-01-maeumjigi-4","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #4] Full-Scale Fine-Tuning Data Preprocessing","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","Artificial Intelligence (AI)-coding","ClaudeCode","capstone","fine-tuning","data-preprocessing","Llama3.1"],"date":"2026-04-01T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #5] Purifying 100,000 Emotion Data Points and the Pressure of Application Programming Interface (API) Costs","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-04-03-maeumjigi-5"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #3] Alarm App Build Test and Fine-Tuning Preprocessing Kickoff","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/maeumjigi-3"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nThis is the record of April 1, when we began processing a massive amount of conversation data into a format suitable for training the Llama 3.1 model, which will become the core engine of the Maeumjigi project.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Automated Execution According to the Preprocessing Plan\\r\\n\\r\\n- **Problem**: To fine-tune the model, the raw data containing user utterances and chatbot responses had to be perfectly converted into a special JSONL format that Llama 3.1 can understand.\\r\\n- **Process**: \\r\\n  > To Claude Code: \\"Proceed with the work according to llama31_finetuning_data_preprocessing_plan_short.md\\"\\r\\n- **Result**: Based on the preprocessing plan document written yesterday, the AI agent independently built and executed the data cleaning and processing pipeline. Instead of a human manually converting tens of thousands of data points one by one, we simply handed over a single Markdown document with the rules and instructed it, \\"Process it exactly like this.\\"\\r\\n\\r\\n### Integrating Category Data and JSONL Conversion\\r\\n\\r\\n- **Problem**: The preprocessing process was broken down into several steps, and finally, all the data had to be combined into one to complete the training dataset.\\r\\n- **Process**:\\r\\n  > To Claude Code: \\"Execute Step 4. Integrate the data of the two categories and convert it to the Llama 3.1 JSONL format.\\"\\r\\n- **Result**: The AI immediately executed Step 4 and merged the scattered conversation data from each category into one. It then wrote and executed a script to convert it into a JSONL file, strictly adhering to the precise prompt template required by Llama 3.1 (differentiating System, User, and Assistant roles).\\r\\n\\r\\n![Terminal Screenshot](./images/02_term.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nToday is the day when \'data preprocessing\', the core of fine-tuning, began to run fully automatically via AI. It was a magical experience to see countless data points transformed into a trainable state with a single instruction: \\"Do it exactly as written in the document.\\"\\r\\nIn the next post, we will continue with exciting dev logs on how we actually train and validate the model using this preprocessed data!"},{"id":"maeumjigi-3","metadata":{"permalink":"/global_blog/blog/maeumjigi-3","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2026-03-31-maeumjigi-3/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-03-31-maeumjigi-3/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #3] Alarm App Build Test and Fine-Tuning Preprocessing Kickoff","description":"Cover Image","date":"2026-03-31T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"Artificial Intelligence (AI)-coding","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/artificial-intelligence-ai-coding"},{"inline":true,"label":"ClaudeCode","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/claude-code"},{"inline":true,"label":"capstone","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/capstone"},{"inline":true,"label":"Android","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/android"},{"inline":true,"label":"build-test","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/build-test"},{"inline":true,"label":"Llama3.1","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/llama-3-1"}],"readingTime":1.72,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"jaybee","title":"Maeumjigi Developer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"maeumjigi-3","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #3] Alarm App Build Test and Fine-Tuning Preprocessing Kickoff","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","Artificial Intelligence (AI)-coding","ClaudeCode","capstone","Android","build-test","Llama3.1"],"date":"2026-03-31T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #4] Full-Scale Fine-Tuning Data Preprocessing","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/2026-04-01-maeumjigi-4"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #2] Initial Alarm App Planning and Testing","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/maeumjigi-2"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nThis is the record of March 31, when we conducted the Android build test of the MND Convention reservation alarm app, which was the initial direction of the Maeumjigi project, while simultaneously starting the **Llama 3.1 fine-tuning data preprocessing**, hinting at a full transition into an AI-based project.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Android App Build and Unit Testing\\r\\n\\r\\n- **Problem**: We needed to verify if the server and Android client codes planned and generated yesterday would successfully build and pass tests in the actual Android Studio environment.\\r\\n- **Process**: \\r\\n  > To Claude Code: \\"Current progress... Stop all work... What were you working on?\\"\\r\\n- **Result**: Numerous build artifacts and metadata were generated in the `mnd-reservation-alarm/android/app/build/...` path. We executed unit tests such as `NotificationBuilderTest` and checked the Android-specific build pipeline, including debug resource merging and manifest processing. The core logic of detecting reservation changes in the background and popping up alarms was tested.\\r\\n\\r\\n### The Great Turning Point: Starting Llama 3.1 Fine-Tuning Preprocessing\\r\\n\\r\\n- **Problem**: Moving beyond a simple alarm app, preparation was needed to integrate an advanced AI that understands the user\'s (soldier\'s) reservation context and emotional state.\\r\\n- **Process**:\\r\\n  > To Claude Code: \\"Please proceed with the work according to llama31_finetuning_data_preprocessing_plan_short.md\\"\\r\\n  > \\"No additional permission is needed for any choices regarding this task.\\"\\r\\n- **Result**: We took a crucial first step that would completely change the nature of the project. We instructed the work according to the data preprocessing plan for fine-tuning the Llama 3.1 model. To speed up the process, we granted the AI strong authority by saying, \\"execute immediately without asking for additional permission for all choices,\\" and started the massive data processing pipeline.\\r\\n\\r\\n![Terminal Screenshot](./images/02_term.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nThis day was a meaningful day when the Android build work to improve the completeness of the alarm app and the AI fine-tuning data preprocessing, which will become the core feature of the future, started in parallel.\\r\\nFrom now on, the arduous preprocessing journey of processing tens of thousands of conversation data points to fit Llama 3.1 will begin in earnest!"},{"id":"maeumjigi-2","metadata":{"permalink":"/global_blog/blog/maeumjigi-2","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2026-03-30-maeumjigi-2/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-03-30-maeumjigi-2/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #2] Initial Alarm App Planning and Testing","description":"Cover Image","date":"2026-03-30T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"Artificial Intelligence (AI)-coding","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/artificial-intelligence-ai-coding"},{"inline":true,"label":"ClaudeCode","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/claude-code"},{"inline":true,"label":"capstone","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/capstone"},{"inline":true,"label":"testing","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/testing"}],"readingTime":1.68,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"jaybee","title":"Maeumjigi Developer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"maeumjigi-2","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #2] Initial Alarm App Planning and Testing","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","Artificial Intelligence (AI)-coding","ClaudeCode","capstone","testing"],"date":"2026-03-30T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #3] Alarm App Build Test and Fine-Tuning Preprocessing Kickoff","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/maeumjigi-3"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #1] The First Steps and Planning","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/maeumjigi-1"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nThis is the record of March 30, when we officially started developing the \'MND Convention Reservation Alarm\' application, which was the initial idea for the Maeumjigi project.\\r\\nBased on the coding rules we set last time, we established a detailed project plan and laid the foundation for Test-Driven Development (TDD).\\r\\n\\r\\n### Designing the Project Milestone\\r\\n\\r\\n- **Problem**: We had the application idea, but we needed a clear step-by-step plan on how to proceed with the development.\\r\\n- **Process**: \\r\\n  > To Claude Code: \\"Please proceed with the project according to Claude plan.md\\"\\r\\n- **Result**: We completed a detailed project plan document called `Claude plan.md`. This document details the implementation goals for each stage, including the Android client, server structure, and crawling, and will serve as an unwavering milestone when working with AI.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Building Test Web Pages (Fixtures)\\r\\n\\r\\n- **Problem**: To build a crawler that detects changes on a reservation site in real-time, we would normally have to continuously connect to the actual website to test it. However, this can strain the server and create an unstable testing environment. Therefore, a mockup environment where we could test safely and freely offline was essential.\\r\\n- **Process**:\\r\\n  > To Claude Code: \\"continue\\"\\r\\n- **Result**: The AI independently determined and perfectly created fake web page (Fixture) HTML data needed for frontend and backend testing. By precisely generating `sample_page.html` (fully booked status) and `sample_page_changed.html` (available status due to cancellations), a strong foundation was laid to quickly and safely test whether the server-side crawler code normally detects changes in HTML DOM elements.\\r\\n\\r\\n![Terminal Screenshot](./images/02_term.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nToday was a day to truly glimpse the beauty of \'AI-driven automated development\', where instead of human step-by-step instructions, we simply told the AI to \\"proceed according to plan,\\" and it independently broke down the milestones and created the necessary test data and documentation.\\r\\nPlease continue to watch our rough (?) journey to see how this simple alarm app project transforms into a massive Deep Learning and Large Language Model (LLM)-based capstone project!"},{"id":"maeumjigi-1","metadata":{"permalink":"/global_blog/blog/maeumjigi-1","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2026-03-26-maeumjigi-1/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-03-26-maeumjigi-1/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #1] The First Steps and Planning","description":"Cover Image","date":"2026-03-26T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"Artificial Intelligence (AI)-coding","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/artificial-intelligence-ai-coding"},{"inline":true,"label":"ClaudeCode","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/claude-code"},{"inline":true,"label":"capstone","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/capstone"},{"inline":true,"label":"project-planning","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/tags/project-planning"}],"readingTime":1.75,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"jaybee","title":"Maeumjigi Developer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"maeumjigi-1","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #1] The First Steps and Planning","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","Artificial Intelligence (AI)-coding","ClaudeCode","capstone","project-planning"],"date":"2026-03-26T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #2] Initial Alarm App Planning and Testing","permalink":"/global_blog/blog/maeumjigi-2"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nThis is the record of March 26, 2026, the very first day of the grand journey of the \\"Maeumjigi\\" capstone project.\\r\\nAt that time, instead of the current AI-based \\"Maeumjigi\\", the project took its first steps simply as an Android alarm application.\\r\\n\\r\\n### The First Encounter with Claude Code\\r\\n\\r\\n- **Process**: The beginning of the project was introducing Claude Code, an AI coding assistant, to our workflow.\\r\\n  > \\"Do you understand Korean?\\"\\r\\n- **Result**: Starting with this light greeting, we began to seriously discuss the system architecture.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Initial Alarm App Planning and Rule Setting\\r\\n\\r\\n- **Problem**: The very first idea we planned was a practical Android application that crawls the reservation status of a specific website (MND Convention) and sends an alarm when an unavailable time slot changes to \'available\'.\\r\\n- **Process**:\\r\\n  > To Claude Code: \\"...I\'m trying to make an app that sends an alarm when a reservation becomes available. Please create a step-by-step plan for making this.\\"\\r\\n  > \\"We do not receive alarms... We will build the Android app according to Claude.md.\\"\\r\\n- **Result**: Based on the initial concept and planning, we wrote `Claude.md` and `CLAUDE_coding_rule.md`, which contained the development direction of the project. In particular, we clearly set the coding conventions and rules that the AI agent must follow when writing code, to prevent any confusion that could occur later when generating massive amounts of code automatically. Although this alarm app plan later took a massive turn into a completely new AI-based capstone project called \'Maeumjigi\', establishing the initial project structure and collaboration rules was a crucial first step. The collaboration document templates created at this time were also very useful later during the large-scale model training process.\\r\\n\\r\\n![Terminal Screenshot](./images/02_term.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nToday, as the first day of Capstone Design, we laid the foundation for planning ideas and communicating with AI. Please stay tuned for our upcoming spectacular series to see how this small and simple Android app plan evolves into a massive Llama fine-tuning and large-scale text synthetic data project! The process of changing the initial plan is also the beauty of Capstone Design."}]}}')}}]);